1
00:00:01,305 --> 00:00:04,534
(Maximum Happiness)

2
00:00:04,535 --> 00:00:06,045
Nice to see you all.

3
00:00:07,275 --> 00:00:09,414
We're going to bring in the star of the Rainbow Live.

4
00:00:09,415 --> 00:00:12,544
A lot of people were surprised that this star would join our show.

5
00:00:12,545 --> 00:00:14,045
I didn't expect her to join our show.

6
00:00:14,185 --> 00:00:15,684
Because she rarely stars on TV shows.

7
00:00:15,685 --> 00:00:17,584
- That's right. - Has anyone crossed paths with her?

8
00:00:17,585 --> 00:00:20,285
I recently met her on a radio show.

9
00:00:20,685 --> 00:00:22,954
She's calmer than I expected.

10
00:00:22,955 --> 00:00:24,494
- She is calm. - That's right.

11
00:00:24,495 --> 00:00:28,165
Actually, I've never met anyone wittier with words than her.

12
00:00:28,225 --> 00:00:29,794
With her natural sense of humor and ability to deliver her acts,

13
00:00:29,795 --> 00:00:32,734
she's an MC, an excellent radio DJ, and a great actress.

14
00:00:32,735 --> 00:00:35,364
She's a true all-rounder who's versatile in many fields.

15
00:00:35,365 --> 00:00:37,235
It's Member Kim Shin Young!

16
00:00:39,575 --> 00:00:41,874
- Hello. - My gosh.

17
00:00:41,875 --> 00:00:42,875
(Welcome to the show.)

18
00:00:42,876 --> 00:00:44,315
(Bowing)

19
00:00:45,815 --> 00:00:47,584
(She became a national sister with her catchphrase, "Brother.")

20
00:00:47,585 --> 00:00:48,785
(Acting cute)

21
00:00:49,885 --> 00:00:51,885
(A woman with 1,000 faces and unparalleled charms)

22
00:00:53,155 --> 00:00:55,524
(A legit comedienne with a solid career of 23 years)

23
00:00:55,525 --> 00:00:56,694
I hope you like my show.

24
00:00:56,695 --> 00:00:59,015
(And her another success, her career as an experienced DJ)

25
00:00:59,295 --> 00:01:00,325
Let's go!

26
00:01:00,495 --> 00:01:02,164
(The best DJ who won numerous awards...)

27
00:01:02,165 --> 00:01:04,795
(and boosted the ratings with her flawless hosting skills)

28
00:01:05,765 --> 00:01:06,934
(She became a muse for Director Park Chan Wook...)

29
00:01:06,935 --> 00:01:08,375
(in his movie, "Decision to Leave.")

30
00:01:08,835 --> 00:01:10,034
(She's a talented actress who won...)

31
00:01:10,035 --> 00:01:11,074
(the Best Supporting Actress...)

32
00:01:11,075 --> 00:01:13,075
(at the Korean Film Producers Association Awards.)

33
00:01:13,275 --> 00:01:14,404
(She used to be an iconic figure of weight-loss 13 years ago...)

34
00:01:14,405 --> 00:01:15,545
(after losing 44kg.)

35
00:01:16,515 --> 00:01:19,645
(She's back in her initial state with her weighty body.)

36
00:01:20,485 --> 00:01:23,855
(The versatile entertainer, Kim Shin Young)

37
00:01:24,185 --> 00:01:25,684
- Gosh, I can't believe this. - Hello.

38
00:01:25,685 --> 00:01:26,954
- Hello. - Welcome.

39
00:01:26,955 --> 00:01:27,984
Please stand in the middle.

40
00:01:27,985 --> 00:01:29,655
- Say hello to our viewers. - Okay.

41
00:01:29,825 --> 00:01:30,895
Hello.

42
00:01:31,255 --> 00:01:33,495
I've been living alone for 14 years.

43
00:01:33,925 --> 00:01:35,794
I'm a comedienne, Kim Shin Young. Nice to meet you.

44
00:01:35,795 --> 00:01:37,434
(Comedienne Kim Shin Young)

45
00:01:37,435 --> 00:01:38,435
(Exhaling)

46
00:01:38,535 --> 00:01:40,011
How does it feel to be in our studio?

47
00:01:40,035 --> 00:01:41,964
It's smaller than I expected.

48
00:01:41,965 --> 00:01:43,375
- Everyone says that. - It is.

49
00:01:43,505 --> 00:01:45,334
I naturally assumed that...

50
00:01:45,335 --> 00:01:47,245
- you had been on the show before. - Okay.

51
00:01:47,345 --> 00:01:48,781
I found out today that this was her first appearance.

52
00:01:48,805 --> 00:01:49,874
- It's my first time here. - Right.

53
00:01:49,875 --> 00:01:51,644
- She rarely comes out of her house. - Right.

54
00:01:51,645 --> 00:01:53,414
- But we're introverts too. - Right.

55
00:01:53,415 --> 00:01:54,615
So you'll feel at ease here.

56
00:01:54,715 --> 00:01:56,814
- I'm an introvert too. - Everyone on our show is calm.

57
00:01:56,815 --> 00:01:57,815
- Really? - Yes.

58
00:01:57,816 --> 00:02:00,325
What made you decide to star on our show?

59
00:02:00,425 --> 00:02:03,625
I always wanted to star on this show at least once.

60
00:02:03,925 --> 00:02:07,465
And I never had a chance to look at my day-to-day life like this.

61
00:02:07,665 --> 00:02:09,164
It's just another day for me.

62
00:02:09,165 --> 00:02:10,394
But I had never observed my days.

63
00:02:10,395 --> 00:02:12,564
So I decided to star on the show.

64
00:02:12,565 --> 00:02:13,704
I was curious about your lifestyle.

65
00:02:13,705 --> 00:02:15,741
She's somewhat of a mystery even in the entertainment industry.

66
00:02:15,765 --> 00:02:17,904
- Right. - I'm curious about her lifestyle.

67
00:02:17,905 --> 00:02:19,805
- I rarely come out of the house. - Right.

68
00:02:20,105 --> 00:02:21,465
She's the biggest homebody I know.

69
00:02:21,645 --> 00:02:23,815
All right. I wonder what she does at home.

70
00:02:24,215 --> 00:02:26,584
She'll reveal her day-to-day life for the first time on TV.

71
00:02:26,585 --> 00:02:28,945
(Let's take a look at Homebody Shin Young's life.)

72
00:02:29,915 --> 00:02:33,225
(Happiness Is Bound to Return)

73
00:02:34,085 --> 00:02:35,254
(Shin Young is sleeping in a dark bedroom.)

74
00:02:35,255 --> 00:02:37,255
- Right, you have blackout curtains. - I do.

75
00:02:38,995 --> 00:02:40,064
(Fascinated)

76
00:02:40,065 --> 00:02:41,395
So that's how I look.

77
00:02:45,365 --> 00:02:48,035
(Throwing)

78
00:02:48,865 --> 00:02:52,635
(She clumsily kicks the pillow.)

79
00:02:53,745 --> 00:02:55,074
I have to stretch.

80
00:02:55,075 --> 00:02:56,244
- That's it? - Yes.

81
00:02:56,245 --> 00:02:58,044
- What? - It was brief, but I did it.

82
00:02:58,045 --> 00:02:59,744
(It was brief.)

83
00:02:59,745 --> 00:03:01,115
It ended in a flash.

84
00:03:01,215 --> 00:03:02,544
In a flash?

85
00:03:02,545 --> 00:03:03,584
She's cute.

86
00:03:03,585 --> 00:03:04,815
(A lightning-speed stretch)

87
00:03:06,015 --> 00:03:11,195
(I should get up now.)

88
00:03:12,655 --> 00:03:15,265
(Struggling)

89
00:03:16,535 --> 00:03:18,294
(She makes the bed as soon as she gets up.)

90
00:03:18,295 --> 00:03:20,975
- You make your bed right away. - I must do it as soon as I get up.

91
00:03:24,275 --> 00:03:25,374
(Spraying)

92
00:03:25,375 --> 00:03:27,075
My goodness. What are you spraying?

93
00:03:27,645 --> 00:03:29,174
Scented mite spray.

94
00:03:29,175 --> 00:03:30,375
- Really? - Yes.

95
00:03:30,515 --> 00:03:32,315
- I also spray that. - It's an absolute must.

96
00:03:32,745 --> 00:03:33,745
The next time I use it,

97
00:03:33,746 --> 00:03:35,144
- the blanket feels fresh. - Right.

98
00:03:35,145 --> 00:03:36,384
- But then... - It's an absolute must.

99
00:03:36,385 --> 00:03:39,115
The dead bodies of the mites must be on your blanket.

100
00:03:39,955 --> 00:03:41,354
- I have a separate pad... - You know?

101
00:03:41,355 --> 00:03:43,355
Underneath my bed.

102
00:03:43,655 --> 00:03:44,655
Really?

103
00:03:44,656 --> 00:03:46,365
Do you have a lot of mites in your house?

104
00:03:46,665 --> 00:03:48,465
I mean, I'm human too.

105
00:03:50,195 --> 00:03:51,664
- Mites... - Right.

106
00:03:51,665 --> 00:03:52,764
- Well, mites... - Do you guys...

107
00:03:52,765 --> 00:03:54,464
talk a lot about mites like this?

108
00:03:54,465 --> 00:03:56,404
We're very interested in other people's lifestyles.

109
00:03:56,405 --> 00:03:57,405
- We are. - Okay.

110
00:03:57,475 --> 00:03:59,474
We'll ask you a lot of questions today like what you spray and so on.

111
00:03:59,475 --> 00:04:01,144
- I'm very interested. - Right.

112
00:04:01,145 --> 00:04:02,944
For some reason, I think she'll have a lot of wise life tips.

113
00:04:02,945 --> 00:04:04,174
Exactly. She's been on her own for 14 years.

114
00:04:04,175 --> 00:04:06,684
- I have many tips. - Do you spray that on here?

115
00:04:06,685 --> 00:04:08,244
- Right. Spray it on your chest. - The mite spray?

116
00:04:08,245 --> 00:04:10,214
I spray it here before I spray it on my blanket.

117
00:04:10,215 --> 00:04:11,554
- I shake them down. - Come on.

118
00:04:11,555 --> 00:04:13,524
(Exterminating mites)

119
00:04:13,525 --> 00:04:14,784
- Hyun Moo. - It's like the Amazon there.

120
00:04:14,785 --> 00:04:16,695
Don't use the spray. Just burn them.

121
00:04:16,795 --> 00:04:18,124
My gosh. Burn them?

122
00:04:18,125 --> 00:04:19,294
- Like this. - No.

123
00:04:19,295 --> 00:04:20,795
I don't get my chest hair waxed.

124
00:04:20,925 --> 00:04:22,495
(Precious)

125
00:04:23,535 --> 00:04:26,535
(After her morning routine of getting rid of mites...)

126
00:04:27,965 --> 00:04:30,305
(Trudging out)

127
00:04:34,245 --> 00:04:35,775
- Gosh. - Her house looks hip.

128
00:04:38,115 --> 00:04:39,614
(A hip living room)

129
00:04:39,615 --> 00:04:40,884
Her living room has a different vibe.

130
00:04:40,885 --> 00:04:42,444
I see a lot of stuff.

131
00:04:42,445 --> 00:04:43,661
(They're spotted in every corner.)

132
00:04:43,685 --> 00:04:44,784
She loves colorful accessories.

133
00:04:44,785 --> 00:04:46,684
- I love colorful accessories. - Yes.

134
00:04:46,685 --> 00:04:48,954
This looks like a room of an idol star.

135
00:04:48,955 --> 00:04:50,594
(Another room in Shin Young's house)

136
00:04:50,595 --> 00:04:51,925
You have so many clothes.

137
00:04:53,125 --> 00:04:55,725
My dream is to turn my dressing room into a clothing showroom.

138
00:04:56,335 --> 00:04:57,695
It does look like a clothing shop.

139
00:04:57,735 --> 00:04:59,805
- Like a clothing shop in Japan. - Right.

140
00:05:00,405 --> 00:05:01,805
- There's more? - Yes.

141
00:05:03,935 --> 00:05:05,574
(The second room after the dressing room)

142
00:05:05,575 --> 00:05:09,145
It's in the back. I call it the Denim Room.

143
00:05:09,305 --> 00:05:10,515
Denim Room?

144
00:05:10,715 --> 00:05:12,595
- My gosh. - She has a separate room for denim?

145
00:05:13,485 --> 00:05:15,284
- You have a Denim Room? - No way.

146
00:05:15,285 --> 00:05:16,515
I love denim.

147
00:05:16,685 --> 00:05:18,784
Any piece of clothing with denim...

148
00:05:18,785 --> 00:05:21,725
- goes straight to the Denim Room. - She has so many clothes.

149
00:05:21,785 --> 00:05:22,954
I was in Japan...

150
00:05:22,955 --> 00:05:24,554
- and shopping for denim clothes. - Yes.

151
00:05:24,555 --> 00:05:26,424
I saw an old denim piece from Brand L.

152
00:05:26,425 --> 00:05:27,965
- Yes. - From Levi's.

153
00:05:28,225 --> 00:05:30,235
It was completely faded,

154
00:05:30,965 --> 00:05:32,564
- but it cost 5,000 dollars. - What?

155
00:05:32,565 --> 00:05:33,604
- Then... - Right.

156
00:05:33,605 --> 00:05:35,434
- it must be from its early days. - It's from the early days.

157
00:05:35,435 --> 00:05:36,774
Why does it cost that much?

158
00:05:36,775 --> 00:05:39,275
The fabric is different.

159
00:05:39,435 --> 00:05:41,344
Each year has different labels too.

160
00:05:41,345 --> 00:05:43,244
They're all different. You're paying for its history.

161
00:05:43,245 --> 00:05:44,975
To me, they were all the same jeans.

162
00:05:45,075 --> 00:05:47,061
The only difference to me was about the depth of color.

163
00:05:47,085 --> 00:05:49,414
Then do you have an expensive denim piece there?

164
00:05:49,415 --> 00:05:50,455
I do.

165
00:05:50,685 --> 00:05:51,885
- I have a piece... - Okay.

166
00:05:52,285 --> 00:05:53,985
- with a five-figure price. - Really?

167
00:05:54,485 --> 00:05:55,755
- Five figures? - Yes.

168
00:05:57,255 --> 00:05:58,455
That's amazing.

169
00:06:00,125 --> 00:06:01,295
So that's the second room.

170
00:06:01,465 --> 00:06:06,165
Then I also have a separate room for shoes which had been my goal.

171
00:06:06,265 --> 00:06:07,604
- For real? - Yes.

172
00:06:07,605 --> 00:06:08,885
Her house is like a select shop.

173
00:06:09,235 --> 00:06:11,034
- Aren't they all limited editions? - Yes.

174
00:06:11,035 --> 00:06:12,434
(All of the walls...)

175
00:06:12,435 --> 00:06:15,205
(are covered with shoes.)

176
00:06:16,275 --> 00:06:18,174
(Nike, Collaboration Zone)

177
00:06:18,175 --> 00:06:19,914
- Oh, that Nike pair! - Which one?

178
00:06:19,915 --> 00:06:21,084
That's an expensive pair.

179
00:06:21,085 --> 00:06:22,984
The first editions are really rare.

180
00:06:22,985 --> 00:06:24,054
Right.

181
00:06:24,055 --> 00:06:25,935
I feel at ease since you know a lot about this.

182
00:06:26,085 --> 00:06:28,255
- I got you today. - Okay.

183
00:06:29,225 --> 00:06:31,624
(They're kept in perfect condition, free of any stains.)

184
00:06:31,625 --> 00:06:34,795
It's really hard to keep them in clean condition like that.

185
00:06:35,265 --> 00:06:36,941
You just collect them, right? You don't wear them.

186
00:06:36,965 --> 00:06:38,395
No. I wear them.

187
00:06:38,835 --> 00:06:40,035
- I do. - They look brand-new.

188
00:06:40,535 --> 00:06:43,504
I rarely step on the ground.

189
00:06:43,505 --> 00:06:45,504
(I rarely step on the ground.)

190
00:06:45,505 --> 00:06:46,505
- What? - What?

191
00:06:46,506 --> 00:06:48,745
- I don't step on dirt. - My gosh.

192
00:06:49,545 --> 00:06:51,714
What's the point of going through that trouble?

193
00:06:51,715 --> 00:06:54,045
They make me happy.

194
00:06:54,515 --> 00:06:55,684
- Hold on. - My gosh.

195
00:06:55,685 --> 00:06:56,914
- They make me happy. - Really?

196
00:06:56,915 --> 00:06:57,955
Of course.

197
00:06:59,655 --> 00:07:01,054
(Yasuhiro Zone)

198
00:07:01,055 --> 00:07:02,755
- Yasuhiro. - Yasuhiro.

199
00:07:03,755 --> 00:07:06,364
- These are handmade sneakers. - You have so many cute pairs.

200
00:07:06,365 --> 00:07:08,525
This is on another level. It's like a museum.

201
00:07:08,765 --> 00:07:10,134
Don't you have thousands of pairs?

202
00:07:10,135 --> 00:07:12,295
I stopped counting the pairs...

203
00:07:12,665 --> 00:07:14,904
- after I hit 700 pairs. - Then you have over 1,000 pairs.

204
00:07:14,905 --> 00:07:16,035
- Probably. - Right.

205
00:07:16,675 --> 00:07:18,635
I have a brother who's three years older than me.

206
00:07:19,105 --> 00:07:22,105
When I was a kid, I had to wear his old sneakers.

207
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:24,175
My parents couldn't afford...

208
00:07:24,415 --> 00:07:26,785
to buy me the sneakers I wanted.

209
00:07:27,215 --> 00:07:31,314
I thought I should buy sneakers when I could afford them.

210
00:07:31,315 --> 00:07:34,054
So I worked at a barbecue restaurant in high school.

211
00:07:34,055 --> 00:07:35,584
I saved up money and bought sneakers.

212
00:07:35,585 --> 00:07:36,894
I bought them in high school.

213
00:07:36,895 --> 00:07:38,494
- That's when you started. - The old pairs. You know, right?

214
00:07:38,495 --> 00:07:40,215
Those old pairs are actually more valuable.

215
00:07:40,325 --> 00:07:41,885
- Right. - There's a story behind that.

216
00:07:42,895 --> 00:07:44,364
I bought them when they were about 300 dollars.

217
00:07:44,365 --> 00:07:45,434
Now, it's in the five-figure price.

218
00:07:45,435 --> 00:07:47,405
- Right. That's crazy valuable now. - Yes.

219
00:07:48,065 --> 00:07:50,705
I could probably buy this house if I were to sell all of them.

220
00:07:52,275 --> 00:07:53,781
But just the thought of selling them...

221
00:07:53,805 --> 00:07:55,944
How can I sell my own babies? No.

222
00:07:55,945 --> 00:07:57,345
(How can I sell my own babies?)

223
00:07:58,645 --> 00:08:01,284
(She goes to Wilson with her phone.)

224
00:08:01,285 --> 00:08:02,815
(Clicking)

225
00:08:05,315 --> 00:08:07,125
It was my first time seeing Wilson in person.

226
00:08:08,085 --> 00:08:09,355
That was a fascinating moment.

227
00:08:12,355 --> 00:08:13,494
Wilson is here.

228
00:08:13,495 --> 00:08:15,095
(I can't believe Wilson is in my house.)

229
00:08:15,625 --> 00:08:18,395
(She goes to the kitchen after a brief meeting with Wilson.)

230
00:08:19,435 --> 00:08:21,365
(Rattling)

231
00:08:23,065 --> 00:08:24,334
- What's that? - Rice?

232
00:08:24,335 --> 00:08:25,405
- Rice? - It's rice.

233
00:08:25,705 --> 00:08:27,445
- You cook at home. - I do.

234
00:08:28,405 --> 00:08:30,945
I have the biggest appetite in the morning.

235
00:08:31,615 --> 00:08:34,484
Let's say I have to wake up at 4am.

236
00:08:34,485 --> 00:08:36,245
Then I must be up by 3am and have my meal.

237
00:08:37,285 --> 00:08:38,584
- Really? - She's so diligent.

238
00:08:38,585 --> 00:08:40,054
- Of course. - Not a lot of people...

239
00:08:40,055 --> 00:08:41,485
have an appetite in the morning.

240
00:08:41,725 --> 00:08:42,754
Not me.

241
00:08:42,755 --> 00:08:45,524
Then you must have a light dinner.

242
00:08:45,525 --> 00:08:46,795
No, I also enjoy my dinner.

243
00:08:47,125 --> 00:08:48,795
I have a huge appetite in the morning.

244
00:08:49,225 --> 00:08:50,765
And dinner is just tasty.

245
00:08:51,495 --> 00:08:52,495
They're different.

246
00:08:52,496 --> 00:08:53,705
Dinner is delicious.

247
00:08:53,865 --> 00:08:55,934
You have an appetite throughout the day.

248
00:08:55,935 --> 00:08:58,575
- I'm busy during lunchtime. - It's the same thing.

249
00:08:59,535 --> 00:09:01,975
(I have a voracious appetite in the morning.)

250
00:09:02,545 --> 00:09:05,345
(Pouring out)

251
00:09:06,615 --> 00:09:08,914
(She gets ready to wash the rice right away.)

252
00:09:08,915 --> 00:09:10,515
She's washing the rice. That's a hassle.

253
00:09:11,715 --> 00:09:13,284
There's the delicious smell...

254
00:09:13,285 --> 00:09:14,884
- of freshly cooked rice. - Yes, I know.

255
00:09:14,885 --> 00:09:18,295
I need my house to smell like freshly cooked rice in the morning.

256
00:09:19,155 --> 00:09:21,425
(She drains the water after the first rinse.)

257
00:09:23,535 --> 00:09:26,335
(She fills it up with water again.)

258
00:09:27,965 --> 00:09:29,705
(Grabbing)

259
00:09:31,635 --> 00:09:35,205
(This time, she pours the water into her tumbler.)

260
00:09:35,305 --> 00:09:39,014
You have to use the water after the third rinse.

261
00:09:39,015 --> 00:09:41,915
If you only rinse it once, it will be a bit stale.

262
00:09:42,945 --> 00:09:45,585
- She sounds like an expert. - She is an expert.

263
00:09:47,255 --> 00:09:52,195
(Putting it in the fridge right away)

264
00:09:54,325 --> 00:09:57,695
(This should do it.)

265
00:09:58,965 --> 00:10:01,504
(After checking the amount of water, she places it in the rice cooker.)

266
00:10:01,505 --> 00:10:03,335
The keep-warm mode is off.

267
00:10:03,475 --> 00:10:04,575
Okay.

268
00:10:05,335 --> 00:10:06,874
I bet you didn't cook...

269
00:10:06,875 --> 00:10:08,804
- when you were on a diet. - I did.

270
00:10:08,805 --> 00:10:10,674
- Then how did you lose weight? - Maybe, she ate less.

271
00:10:10,675 --> 00:10:12,215
No. I don't add brown rice anymore.

272
00:10:12,315 --> 00:10:13,955
I used to mix brown rice with white rice.

273
00:10:14,515 --> 00:10:15,584
I stopped mixing brown rice...

274
00:10:15,585 --> 00:10:17,054
- and have been eating white rice. - You eat white rice.

275
00:10:17,055 --> 00:10:18,214
And rice tastes so good.

276
00:10:18,215 --> 00:10:19,654
- White rice? - It melts in my mouth.

277
00:10:19,655 --> 00:10:20,924
- Is white rice that fattening? - I don't even have to chew.

278
00:10:20,925 --> 00:10:21,984
I can just swallow it.

279
00:10:21,985 --> 00:10:23,455
- I bet. - My gosh. No way.

280
00:10:23,695 --> 00:10:25,324
When you start craving white rice,

281
00:10:25,325 --> 00:10:27,424
it's a sign that you'll gain 5kg.

282
00:10:27,425 --> 00:10:29,495
Right. White rice is tasty, but it's fattening.

283
00:10:30,465 --> 00:10:32,395
(Opening)

284
00:10:33,405 --> 00:10:35,264
(Her fridge is neatly organized.)

285
00:10:35,265 --> 00:10:37,425
- You have a lot of side dishes. - My fridge is clean.

286
00:10:39,005 --> 00:10:41,805
(Reaching)

287
00:10:42,375 --> 00:10:45,145
(She grabs a side dish container without hesitation.)

288
00:10:45,815 --> 00:10:47,515
- I'm curious what she'll eat. - So...

289
00:10:48,685 --> 00:10:51,385
(Rummaging through)

290
00:10:52,885 --> 00:10:54,184
(She places a pot on the stove first.)

291
00:10:54,185 --> 00:10:55,325
Oh, right.

292
00:10:57,195 --> 00:10:59,625
(She opens the side dish container.)

293
00:11:00,795 --> 00:11:01,915
- What's that? - What is it?

294
00:11:02,525 --> 00:11:04,494
- My gosh. That looks so good. - For breakfast?

295
00:11:04,495 --> 00:11:05,634
You marinated it beforehand.

296
00:11:05,635 --> 00:11:07,341
(Here's beef bulgogi she marinated the day before.)

297
00:11:07,365 --> 00:11:09,335
(Humming)

298
00:11:10,275 --> 00:11:12,805
I always prepare my breakfast late at night the day before.

299
00:11:13,105 --> 00:11:16,045
If I'm craving bulgogi, I marinate some beef.

300
00:11:16,345 --> 00:11:18,614
I even make zucchini jeon the day before too.

301
00:11:18,615 --> 00:11:21,785
So I can take them out next morning and eat my breakfast right away.

302
00:11:22,145 --> 00:11:25,385
My evening has to be exhausting to have a happy morning.

303
00:11:25,555 --> 00:11:27,055
She's so diligent.

304
00:11:28,085 --> 00:11:31,295
(While thanking yesterday's me...)

305
00:11:31,495 --> 00:11:33,994
(for making this morning's me happy...)

306
00:11:33,995 --> 00:11:35,064
I've never seen anyone who lives alone...

307
00:11:35,065 --> 00:11:36,105
marinate beef for bulgogi.

308
00:11:37,035 --> 00:11:39,695
I must eat a lot of meat for breakfast.

309
00:11:41,165 --> 00:11:45,035
(She transfers a lot of marinated beef to the pot.)

310
00:11:45,935 --> 00:11:47,805
(Generously pouring the sauce)

311
00:11:48,105 --> 00:11:49,781
Do you add any special ingredient to the marinade?

312
00:11:49,805 --> 00:11:50,875
I add apple juice.

313
00:11:51,445 --> 00:11:53,644
I drizzle apple juice over the meat.

314
00:11:53,645 --> 00:11:56,655
That way, it tenderizes the meat before adding the marinade.

315
00:11:57,415 --> 00:11:59,055
Besides, people eat apples for breakfast.

316
00:12:00,085 --> 00:12:02,255
- So you added it to the marinade? - Right.

317
00:12:02,555 --> 00:12:03,794
- Don't try to convince us. - For breakfast.

318
00:12:03,795 --> 00:12:05,155
(Her logic almost convinced them.)

319
00:12:05,595 --> 00:12:08,165
(She puts her bulgogi that makes perfect sense on the stove.)

320
00:12:08,865 --> 00:12:11,735
(Pulling it out)

321
00:12:12,035 --> 00:12:14,834
There's a sense of certainty in all of her movements.

322
00:12:14,835 --> 00:12:16,875
(Laughing)

323
00:12:17,635 --> 00:12:18,734
This too.

324
00:12:18,735 --> 00:12:21,945
(She grabs the containers without a moment of hesitation.)

325
00:12:22,805 --> 00:12:27,415
(She comes back with both hands full of side dishes.)

326
00:12:28,785 --> 00:12:29,814
(Cucumber kimchi to boost her appetite)

327
00:12:29,815 --> 00:12:31,654
- Cucumber kimchi? - Yes, cucumber kimchi.

328
00:12:31,655 --> 00:12:32,684
- Did you... - She's amazing.

329
00:12:32,685 --> 00:12:34,124
- I love it. - Seaweed stems.

330
00:12:34,125 --> 00:12:36,406
- They're so good too. - You eat them when it's just you?

331
00:12:38,125 --> 00:12:42,125
(She puts back the side dish containers.)

332
00:12:44,165 --> 00:12:46,295
(Careful)

333
00:12:46,905 --> 00:12:48,965
- I'm very careful with it. - What's that?

334
00:12:49,135 --> 00:12:50,475
What is it? My gosh.

335
00:12:51,705 --> 00:12:54,405
My gosh, it smells so good.

336
00:12:55,775 --> 00:12:57,344
(Looking delicious in no time)

337
00:12:57,345 --> 00:12:59,074
- The house must smell like bulgogi. - Right.

338
00:12:59,075 --> 00:13:00,885
On top of the rice getting cooked.

339
00:13:02,745 --> 00:13:05,515
(Opening)

340
00:13:07,085 --> 00:13:08,685
- Glass noodles? - What?

341
00:13:09,625 --> 00:13:11,295
- To put them in the bulgogi? - Yes.

342
00:13:11,825 --> 00:13:13,924
Even restaurants wouldn't serve such heavy meals in the morning.

343
00:13:13,925 --> 00:13:16,171
(Even restaurants wouldn't serve such heavy meals in the morning.)

344
00:13:16,195 --> 00:13:17,795
I must always have glass noodles.

345
00:13:18,165 --> 00:13:20,105
They must be cooked the moment I put them in.

346
00:13:20,305 --> 00:13:23,305
Dried-up glass noodles will need time to be cooked.

347
00:13:23,675 --> 00:13:25,604
I had this obsession back in the day.

348
00:13:25,605 --> 00:13:27,565
"I should eat glass noodles instead of eating..."

349
00:13:27,845 --> 00:13:29,105
"any other noodles or ramyeon."

350
00:13:29,405 --> 00:13:31,345
But it's better to eat ramyeon.

351
00:13:31,975 --> 00:13:34,315
Once you get hooked on glass noodles, you eat too much.

352
00:13:35,185 --> 00:13:37,085
I even add glass noodles to ramyeon.

353
00:13:37,615 --> 00:13:39,555
So I can't stop eating them.

354
00:13:39,885 --> 00:13:40,955
There's nothing I can do.

355
00:13:41,155 --> 00:13:42,354
Glass noodles are tasty...

356
00:13:42,355 --> 00:13:44,355
- in every dish. - Right.

357
00:13:45,055 --> 00:13:47,925
(She washes her hands thoroughly first.)

358
00:13:49,895 --> 00:13:53,295
(Grabbing a handful of glass noodles)

359
00:13:55,105 --> 00:13:57,175
(Adding them right into the pot)

360
00:13:57,975 --> 00:13:59,675
That's quite fattening.

361
00:13:59,835 --> 00:14:01,674
It's okay. They're made of sweet potato starch.

362
00:14:01,675 --> 00:14:02,775
(Don't worry.)

363
00:14:03,845 --> 00:14:05,275
I can't get used to this fridge.

364
00:14:08,385 --> 00:14:10,815
(Adding the rice-rinsed water)

365
00:14:11,785 --> 00:14:13,455
(I can't forget about this.)

366
00:14:14,085 --> 00:14:15,855
(Reaching)

367
00:14:18,195 --> 00:14:20,024
- It's black pepper. - Black pepper?

368
00:14:20,025 --> 00:14:21,324
My gosh. That must be really tasty.

369
00:14:21,325 --> 00:14:22,564
- Wait. Black pepper? - My gosh.

370
00:14:22,565 --> 00:14:23,735
I'm Black Pepper Man.

371
00:14:24,635 --> 00:14:27,265
(I'm Black Pepper Man.)

372
00:14:27,605 --> 00:14:28,734
- The problem is I add too much. - I bet you hate ground pepper.

373
00:14:28,735 --> 00:14:30,135
- I hate that. - The ground pepper.

374
00:14:30,275 --> 00:14:32,175
That's useless. My gosh.

375
00:14:32,375 --> 00:14:33,434
That's the OG black pepper.

376
00:14:33,435 --> 00:14:35,574
- That's the OG black pepper. - That's the OG black pepper.

377
00:14:35,575 --> 00:14:37,615
- That's it. - The finely ground black pepper.

378
00:14:38,545 --> 00:14:40,445
(Generously sprinkling black pepper)

379
00:14:40,815 --> 00:14:42,315
My gosh, this looks good.

380
00:14:44,855 --> 00:14:47,131
Not a lot of people get to hear the rice cooker in the morning.

381
00:14:47,155 --> 00:14:48,255
Seriously.

382
00:14:48,725 --> 00:14:49,784
But it's a lovely sound.

383
00:14:49,785 --> 00:14:51,095
- Right? - Yes.

384
00:14:53,095 --> 00:14:56,095
(Let me check the seasoning.)

385
00:14:57,065 --> 00:14:58,235
Nice.

386
00:14:59,035 --> 00:15:00,535
Pho needs cilantro.

387
00:15:00,965 --> 00:15:02,905
- Stew needs black pepper. - Right.

388
00:15:05,375 --> 00:15:06,435
That's good.

389
00:15:08,545 --> 00:15:11,114
(She washes the used pot immediately.)

390
00:15:11,115 --> 00:15:12,115
She keeps it clean.

391
00:15:13,045 --> 00:15:14,685
(Cleaning)

392
00:15:16,615 --> 00:15:18,085
You can even use her sink to cook.

393
00:15:19,915 --> 00:15:22,824
I use toothpaste and leftover salt to clean the sink.

394
00:15:22,825 --> 00:15:24,985
- It's spotless then. - There aren't any water stains.

395
00:15:25,625 --> 00:15:27,865
(Grabbing)

396
00:15:30,795 --> 00:15:31,865
- What? - What's that?

397
00:15:32,295 --> 00:15:33,494
- What is that? - My gosh.

398
00:15:33,495 --> 00:15:34,564
- Seaweed soup. - Yes.

399
00:15:34,565 --> 00:15:35,604
(Here's a dried block of seaweed soup.)

400
00:15:35,605 --> 00:15:36,665
- I see. - The soup is...

401
00:15:37,035 --> 00:15:38,481
You need some kind of soup no matter what.

402
00:15:38,505 --> 00:15:39,605
I need soup.

403
00:15:41,845 --> 00:15:44,645
(Her meal is almost ready.)

404
00:15:45,715 --> 00:15:48,185
(Staring)

405
00:15:49,345 --> 00:15:51,285
(Pouting)

406
00:15:52,115 --> 00:15:53,185
Fourteen minutes.

407
00:15:57,425 --> 00:15:59,145
The rice is taking longer than she thought.

408
00:15:59,295 --> 00:16:01,625
- The timing was off. - I see.

409
00:16:02,895 --> 00:16:03,965
Fourteen minutes.

410
00:16:04,595 --> 00:16:06,465
(Scowling)

411
00:16:07,835 --> 00:16:09,665
But what if the glass noodles get soggy?

412
00:16:10,505 --> 00:16:12,504
I was so conflicted.

413
00:16:12,505 --> 00:16:14,081
(A mental game against the rice cooker over glass noodles)

414
00:16:14,105 --> 00:16:16,405
(A suffocating confrontation)

415
00:16:18,345 --> 00:16:19,645
(Both of them are steaming.)

416
00:16:22,945 --> 00:16:25,085
- What's going on? - She might smack the rice cooker.

417
00:16:25,255 --> 00:16:26,815
(Sensitive)

418
00:16:28,485 --> 00:16:30,555
(Time keeps passing amidst growing anxiety.)

419
00:16:31,595 --> 00:16:33,454
(Her grand meal is getting cold without the rice.)

420
00:16:33,455 --> 00:16:35,015
She's waiting until the rice is cooked.

421
00:16:35,095 --> 00:16:37,734
- I would've eaten the bulgogi. - Same here.

422
00:16:37,735 --> 00:16:38,765
Me too.

423
00:16:39,895 --> 00:16:41,565
My gosh. It's taking way too long.

424
00:16:42,035 --> 00:16:43,105
I started too early.

425
00:16:45,935 --> 00:16:48,505
(She plops down on her chair, exhausted from hunger.)

426
00:16:49,375 --> 00:16:51,915
(Yes. Let me pick out a video first.)

427
00:16:53,715 --> 00:16:54,915
Is it baseball?

428
00:16:55,285 --> 00:16:56,885
It was a highlight video from yesterday.

429
00:16:58,315 --> 00:16:59,784
(Stirring)

430
00:16:59,785 --> 00:17:00,855
She's going to eat.

431
00:17:01,855 --> 00:17:03,584
- I ended up eating them first. - Glass noodles first.

432
00:17:03,585 --> 00:17:05,555
- They get soggy. - Right.

433
00:17:07,725 --> 00:17:10,965
(Slurping)

434
00:17:11,835 --> 00:17:14,635
(Yes, this is what I'm talking about.)

435
00:17:17,435 --> 00:17:18,704
Gosh, it looks so good.

436
00:17:18,705 --> 00:17:19,875
My gosh, it's good.

437
00:17:21,975 --> 00:17:24,245
(Getting a thrill)

438
00:17:25,375 --> 00:17:27,335
I ate the noodles first before my rice was ready.

439
00:17:27,685 --> 00:17:30,785
The glass noodles steal all the sauce.

440
00:17:30,985 --> 00:17:33,485
So I started with the glass noodles.

441
00:17:33,615 --> 00:17:34,685
It's a palate cleanser.

442
00:17:35,155 --> 00:17:36,485
Like a dessert.

443
00:17:37,095 --> 00:17:38,624
It's like a dessert?

444
00:17:38,625 --> 00:17:40,001
(She's enjoying her dessert, the glass noodles, in the morning.)

445
00:17:40,025 --> 00:17:41,424
Isn't that an appetizer?

446
00:17:41,425 --> 00:17:44,865
Actually, desserts should be eaten before the meal.

447
00:17:45,135 --> 00:17:46,395
Meals come after desserts.

448
00:17:47,165 --> 00:17:48,404
- What? - That's how it is.

449
00:17:48,405 --> 00:17:50,704
- Some do that to lose weight. - The order of the meal... Right.

450
00:17:50,705 --> 00:17:52,865
- To delay the insulin spike? - Desserts first... Yes.

451
00:17:53,005 --> 00:17:54,674
Do you only eat that type of glass noodles?

452
00:17:54,675 --> 00:17:56,344
- Only those glass noodles. - The old-school one?

453
00:17:56,345 --> 00:17:57,444
Yes, the old-school one.

454
00:17:57,445 --> 00:18:00,214
- Wide ones don't taste the same. - I know. Wide ones...

455
00:18:00,215 --> 00:18:01,744
- The ones that go into tteokbokki? - Yes.

456
00:18:01,745 --> 00:18:03,954
Wide glass noodles can't soak up the sauce well.

457
00:18:03,955 --> 00:18:06,084
- Even when you soak them in water, - Right.

458
00:18:06,085 --> 00:18:07,425
I can finish them in a few bites.

459
00:18:08,525 --> 00:18:09,554
They become the star of the dish.

460
00:18:09,555 --> 00:18:12,855
Here's what I noticed about foodies.

461
00:18:12,925 --> 00:18:14,294
I thought they'd be accepting of all food.

462
00:18:14,295 --> 00:18:15,624
But they discriminate a lot.

463
00:18:15,625 --> 00:18:17,494
- Right. We're very picky. - People...

464
00:18:17,495 --> 00:18:18,864
- Discriminate? - People...

465
00:18:18,865 --> 00:18:21,635
who aren't foodies would just eat anything.

466
00:18:21,805 --> 00:18:24,125
We've tried all kinds of food and then found the best one.

467
00:18:24,335 --> 00:18:26,134
- The best one. - We want the best for our meal.

468
00:18:26,135 --> 00:18:27,304
You went through trial and error.

469
00:18:27,305 --> 00:18:29,375
Of course. I've gone through trial and error.

470
00:18:32,075 --> 00:18:35,685
(Getting a refill of her best glass noodles)

471
00:18:39,415 --> 00:18:42,285
(Engrossed in the food)

472
00:18:43,355 --> 00:18:45,095
(Grabbing)

473
00:18:47,465 --> 00:18:49,425
(Crunchy)

474
00:18:50,365 --> 00:18:53,295
(Refreshing cucumber kimchi as the palate cleanser)

475
00:18:54,005 --> 00:18:56,865
(Humming)

476
00:18:57,675 --> 00:18:58,705
I'm hungry.

477
00:19:00,405 --> 00:19:01,405
I'm hungry.

478
00:19:04,045 --> 00:19:05,974
- You're having it without the rice. - I was hungry.

479
00:19:05,975 --> 00:19:07,445
- So it's Round One. - Yes.

480
00:19:09,545 --> 00:19:13,115
Your delicious glutinous white rice is ready.

481
00:19:14,855 --> 00:19:16,655
- At last. - My rice is cooked.

482
00:19:17,055 --> 00:19:18,124
Great timing.

483
00:19:18,125 --> 00:19:20,095
(At last!)

484
00:19:21,025 --> 00:19:23,765
(Getting up)

485
00:19:24,965 --> 00:19:26,934
I like having clean white rice.

486
00:19:26,935 --> 00:19:28,615
- So the sauce doesn't get on it? - Right.

487
00:19:29,635 --> 00:19:32,005
(She finally opens the rice cooker.)

488
00:19:32,475 --> 00:19:34,005
Nice.

489
00:19:36,975 --> 00:19:38,574
(She finally sees her white rice after a long wait.)

490
00:19:38,575 --> 00:19:40,175
I love the smell of freshly cooked rice.

491
00:19:41,385 --> 00:19:42,685
(Praying)

492
00:19:44,955 --> 00:19:46,814
(Feeling solemn before the white rice)

493
00:19:46,815 --> 00:19:47,924
She's solemn.

494
00:19:47,925 --> 00:19:49,445
(Feeling solemn before the white rice)

495
00:19:51,095 --> 00:19:53,425
(Stirring)

496
00:19:54,565 --> 00:19:58,265
(Dividing them into four sections in a sacred manner)

497
00:19:59,265 --> 00:20:00,505
(Blowing)

498
00:20:02,905 --> 00:20:05,305
Look at it. It's glistening and looks deliciously sticky.

499
00:20:07,375 --> 00:20:09,845
(In a good mood)

500
00:20:11,015 --> 00:20:12,474
I'll start my meal at last.

501
00:20:12,475 --> 00:20:13,835
(Her breakfast begins in earnest.)

502
00:20:14,685 --> 00:20:17,045
(Pulling closer)

503
00:20:18,015 --> 00:20:20,625
(She's been looking forward to the white rice.)

504
00:20:23,025 --> 00:20:24,695
It does look really good.

505
00:20:24,895 --> 00:20:26,155
(Raised eyebrows)

506
00:20:28,195 --> 00:20:30,124
(It's so good that her eyes open wide.)

507
00:20:30,125 --> 00:20:31,234
The rice came out well.

508
00:20:31,235 --> 00:20:32,915
I could finally see what's in front of me.

509
00:20:33,235 --> 00:20:36,405
(She enjoys the classic combo, bulgogi with the rice.)

510
00:20:40,805 --> 00:20:45,415
(She's enjoying her meal with each side dish.)

511
00:20:48,215 --> 00:20:49,915
(Slurping)

512
00:20:51,085 --> 00:20:52,454
She's eating with gusto.

513
00:20:52,455 --> 00:20:54,024
I bet moms adore you.

514
00:20:54,025 --> 00:20:55,655
(Quickly eating her meal with gusto)

515
00:20:56,725 --> 00:20:58,995
(She has a sip of the warm seaweed soup.)

516
00:20:59,295 --> 00:21:01,395
(Satisfied)

517
00:21:01,495 --> 00:21:03,695
(Satisfied)

518
00:21:05,065 --> 00:21:06,864
(Drinking up the seaweed soup as if it's unrefined makgeolli)

519
00:21:06,865 --> 00:21:09,265
Why did they add a clip of Mo Pal Mo?

520
00:21:10,875 --> 00:21:12,675
(A satisfied sigh)

521
00:21:13,135 --> 00:21:14,645
(Gathering)

522
00:21:15,545 --> 00:21:18,215
(She's almost done with bulgogi in no time.)

523
00:21:21,685 --> 00:21:22,945
- You'll mix rice in it? - Yes.

524
00:21:23,545 --> 00:21:24,885
So it's easier to wash them.

525
00:21:25,755 --> 00:21:27,254
(Round 3, seaweed rice soup)

526
00:21:27,255 --> 00:21:28,975
- Gosh, that looks good. - That looks good.

527
00:21:31,595 --> 00:21:33,324
Having it with kimchi...

528
00:21:33,325 --> 00:21:34,595
It's so good.

529
00:21:34,825 --> 00:21:36,025
(Salivating)

530
00:21:36,995 --> 00:21:41,465
(Gobbling up the food as if it's her first bite)

531
00:21:43,105 --> 00:21:44,635
(Hitting the spot)

532
00:21:45,135 --> 00:21:47,704
I went on a date with bulgogi and rice.

533
00:21:47,705 --> 00:21:50,575
Then I finished it with seaweed soup.

534
00:21:50,675 --> 00:21:52,374
It was an alms bowl meal.

535
00:21:52,375 --> 00:21:53,845
(It was an alms bowl meal.)

536
00:21:54,085 --> 00:21:55,445
People don't change.

537
00:21:55,745 --> 00:21:59,255
Some say our constitution can change. That's all nonsense.

538
00:21:59,785 --> 00:22:00,954
We always come back to it.

539
00:22:00,955 --> 00:22:02,685
(We always come back to it in the end.)

540
00:22:02,885 --> 00:22:04,194
What's the point of disciplining myself for 13 years?

541
00:22:04,195 --> 00:22:05,255
It took three months.

542
00:22:06,295 --> 00:22:07,695
- You gained weight fast. - Right.

543
00:22:09,495 --> 00:22:10,934
I was like, "What's life?"

544
00:22:10,935 --> 00:22:14,535
A chocolate cake was the starting point.

545
00:22:15,165 --> 00:22:17,004
I eat whatever I crave.

546
00:22:17,005 --> 00:22:18,205
I eat when I get hungry.

547
00:22:19,705 --> 00:22:22,504
Back in the day, I was sensitive.

548
00:22:22,505 --> 00:22:24,015
But I'm easygoing now.

549
00:22:24,175 --> 00:22:26,315
Even when people step on my toes,

550
00:22:26,515 --> 00:22:27,985
I wouldn't get so mad.

551
00:22:28,345 --> 00:22:30,045
I've become more generous.

552
00:22:30,755 --> 00:22:31,914
You've gotten gentle.

553
00:22:31,915 --> 00:22:34,224
- She definitely changed. - Yes.

554
00:22:34,225 --> 00:22:35,624
You were an iconic figure of losing weight...

555
00:22:35,625 --> 00:22:37,054
up until a few years ago.

556
00:22:37,055 --> 00:22:38,224
- Right. - Yes.

557
00:22:38,225 --> 00:22:39,694
She used to say that she had to lose weight...

558
00:22:39,695 --> 00:22:42,294
- to live a healthy life. - Right.

559
00:22:42,295 --> 00:22:44,434
- Seriously. - My body might be healthy.

560
00:22:44,435 --> 00:22:47,065
- But my mind wasn't so healthy. - I see.

561
00:22:47,135 --> 00:22:49,364
I do respect you for this, though.

562
00:22:49,365 --> 00:22:50,604
- What? - You lost weight...

563
00:22:50,605 --> 00:22:51,634
during the toughest time...

564
00:22:51,635 --> 00:22:53,834
- to lose weight. - Right.

565
00:22:53,835 --> 00:22:55,074
- It's easier to lose weight now. - Right.

566
00:22:55,075 --> 00:22:56,144
- But you won't take the easy way. - Right.

567
00:22:56,145 --> 00:22:57,674
- Exactly. - You're right.

568
00:22:57,675 --> 00:22:58,714
- You're right. - Yes.

569
00:22:58,715 --> 00:23:00,244
- I... - You're so sharp.

570
00:23:00,245 --> 00:23:02,644
- There are Wegovy and Mounjaro. - Right. Mounjaro.

571
00:23:02,645 --> 00:23:04,014
- Wegovy. - I don't want to take them.

572
00:23:04,015 --> 00:23:05,354
You're not tempted to take them?

573
00:23:05,355 --> 00:23:06,684
No temptations.

574
00:23:06,685 --> 00:23:08,824
- You're not tempted to take them? - No temptations.

575
00:23:08,825 --> 00:23:09,924
- But what made you... - Really?

576
00:23:09,925 --> 00:23:13,154
- decide to start bulking up? - Yes.

577
00:23:13,155 --> 00:23:14,695
- Bulking up... - Bulk up?

578
00:23:15,025 --> 00:23:17,095
- I didn't mean it like that. - Hold on.

579
00:23:17,435 --> 00:23:19,334
- So is this how it goes here? - No.

580
00:23:19,335 --> 00:23:21,364
- Is this "Show Me the Money?" - No.

581
00:23:21,365 --> 00:23:22,805
His diss was ruthless.

582
00:23:24,535 --> 00:23:26,604
Everyone has a good heart.

583
00:23:26,605 --> 00:23:27,804
- Really? - They're not eloquent.

584
00:23:27,805 --> 00:23:29,104
- Are we here to diss each other? - He's a good guy.

585
00:23:29,105 --> 00:23:30,714
No. People bulk up first...

586
00:23:30,715 --> 00:23:32,474
- before they build muscles. - Why...

587
00:23:32,475 --> 00:23:34,515
Why would I need to build muscles?

588
00:23:35,685 --> 00:23:37,284
- He didn't mean it like that. - Okay.

589
00:23:37,285 --> 00:23:39,214
- I didn't mean to bulk up. - Okay.

590
00:23:39,215 --> 00:23:40,755
Actually, I stayed...

591
00:23:40,855 --> 00:23:43,124
by the late professor's side until his last moment.

592
00:23:43,125 --> 00:23:44,554
- I'm talking about Jeon Yu Seong. - Jeon Yu Seong.

593
00:23:44,555 --> 00:23:47,725
The professor said, "Hey, Shin Young."

594
00:23:48,195 --> 00:23:50,024
"I'm really craving jjamppong."

595
00:23:50,025 --> 00:23:51,895
"But I can't eat that now."

596
00:23:52,165 --> 00:23:54,341
Then he said, "Don't waste your life denying yourself some good food."

597
00:23:54,365 --> 00:23:56,434
"Eat whatever you want."

598
00:23:56,435 --> 00:23:58,334
What he said resonated with me.

599
00:23:58,335 --> 00:24:01,444
- I had been on a diet for 14 years. - Right.

600
00:24:01,445 --> 00:24:02,604
I'd say things, "No. Not now."

601
00:24:02,605 --> 00:24:03,774
- "I can't eat now." - Right.

602
00:24:03,775 --> 00:24:06,175
"No bread for me." He saw me turn down food.

603
00:24:06,245 --> 00:24:08,984
Toward the end, he told me to eat whatever I craved.

604
00:24:08,985 --> 00:24:10,044
- He was right about that. - Right.

605
00:24:10,045 --> 00:24:11,414
- Yes. - I decided not to be bothered...

606
00:24:11,415 --> 00:24:12,614
by gaining weight. That's just me.

607
00:24:12,615 --> 00:24:14,054
- I should still love myself. - Right.

608
00:24:14,055 --> 00:24:16,985
So I ate everything I wanted.

609
00:24:17,495 --> 00:24:19,094
That was the reason behind your story.

610
00:24:19,095 --> 00:24:20,864
Right. I had a backstory to it.

611
00:24:20,865 --> 00:24:23,895
(A heartwarming backstory to her weight gain)

612
00:24:25,195 --> 00:24:26,265
That was good.

613
00:24:28,865 --> 00:24:30,505
(Rattling)

614
00:24:30,775 --> 00:24:32,375
(Wiping)

615
00:24:33,975 --> 00:24:35,105
She's so clean.

616
00:24:35,445 --> 00:24:36,774
I must clean them right away.

617
00:24:36,775 --> 00:24:37,775
(She wipes the table while clearing the table.)

618
00:24:37,776 --> 00:24:40,045
- I must clean them right away. - That's impressive.

619
00:24:43,415 --> 00:24:45,584
That's one of the reasons I don't cook.

620
00:24:45,585 --> 00:24:47,824
- Cleaning up is such a hassle. - It's a hassle.

621
00:24:47,825 --> 00:24:49,884
- It's a big hassle. - A lot of people say that.

622
00:24:49,885 --> 00:24:51,454
- If you do it right away, it's not. - Right.

623
00:24:51,455 --> 00:24:52,455
- Really? - Yes.

624
00:24:52,725 --> 00:24:55,765
(Let's see.)

625
00:24:59,335 --> 00:25:01,135
- My gosh. - Nice.

626
00:25:01,705 --> 00:25:04,805
- It's not good once it dries up. - Right.

627
00:25:04,905 --> 00:25:07,545
(She tightly wraps up the leftover side dish.)

628
00:25:08,275 --> 00:25:09,344
(She puts it in the fridge.)

629
00:25:09,345 --> 00:25:11,174
Did you run a restaurant before?

630
00:25:11,175 --> 00:25:13,675
I worked part-time at a jjajangmyeon restaurant.

631
00:25:13,775 --> 00:25:15,044
When I worked there,

632
00:25:15,045 --> 00:25:16,744
- I had to rip it with this hand. - The pickled radishes.

633
00:25:16,745 --> 00:25:17,884
You used your hand blade?

634
00:25:17,885 --> 00:25:19,485
I used my hand blade.

635
00:25:19,885 --> 00:25:21,324
Using scissors would take too long.

636
00:25:21,325 --> 00:25:22,554
- It takes too long. - You wrapped the food...

637
00:25:22,555 --> 00:25:23,624
- and went on delivery runs? - Of course.

638
00:25:23,625 --> 00:25:24,824
I'd wrap it up and deliver it.

639
00:25:24,825 --> 00:25:25,994
I'd wrap up pickled radishes...

640
00:25:25,995 --> 00:25:27,154
- in the morning. - Of course.

641
00:25:27,155 --> 00:25:29,225
Then I'd grab one and deliver the food.

642
00:25:29,265 --> 00:25:31,365
I'm quite good at driving a motorcycle.

643
00:25:31,695 --> 00:25:33,034
You drove a motorcycle to deliver food?

644
00:25:33,035 --> 00:25:34,264
I used to deliver food too.

645
00:25:34,265 --> 00:25:35,404
(Impressed)

646
00:25:35,405 --> 00:25:36,934
You're a true all-rounder.

647
00:25:36,935 --> 00:25:38,005
(Amazed)

648
00:25:38,635 --> 00:25:40,735
(Water gushing out)

649
00:25:41,845 --> 00:25:44,144
(She washes the bowls and plates she used right away.)

650
00:25:44,145 --> 00:25:45,514
You're washing them with bare hands.

651
00:25:45,515 --> 00:25:46,614
- Right. - Without any gloves.

652
00:25:46,615 --> 00:25:48,844
- I can't check if they're clean. - Right.

653
00:25:48,845 --> 00:25:50,145
(Squeaky clean)

654
00:25:50,985 --> 00:25:53,115
It became a habit of mine.

655
00:25:53,485 --> 00:25:55,085
I'll be the one cleaning up.

656
00:25:55,685 --> 00:25:56,984
I'll be the one throwing out the trash.

657
00:25:56,985 --> 00:25:58,655
After watching TV...

658
00:25:58,895 --> 00:26:00,925
and wasting my spare time,

659
00:26:01,195 --> 00:26:03,365
it gets really hard to finish all my tasks at once.

660
00:26:03,625 --> 00:26:07,165
Thinking about these tasks is stressful.

661
00:26:07,365 --> 00:26:09,781
I'll have to do them anyway. Getting them done fast is the best.

662
00:26:09,805 --> 00:26:12,305
When I can't get out of it, it's better to finish it fast.

663
00:26:13,575 --> 00:26:15,205
(Turning off)

664
00:26:15,745 --> 00:26:18,115
That was fast. She washes the dishes so fast.

665
00:26:19,945 --> 00:26:21,285
I'm done.

666
00:26:23,915 --> 00:26:25,055
What will you do next?

667
00:26:25,185 --> 00:26:27,455
I'm very busy in the morning.

668
00:26:27,485 --> 00:26:31,025
(Going through the upper cupboard)

669
00:26:31,695 --> 00:26:33,025
(She takes out containers.)

670
00:26:33,055 --> 00:26:34,365
My gosh. I forgot.

671
00:26:35,125 --> 00:26:37,225
(She opens up the rice cooker.)

672
00:26:37,325 --> 00:26:38,664
- I divide it into containers. - Rice?

673
00:26:38,665 --> 00:26:39,794
- Right. - I see.

674
00:26:39,795 --> 00:26:41,064
- Really? - Yes.

675
00:26:41,065 --> 00:26:43,164
- You don't keep it in the cooker? - Or the rice gets dry.

676
00:26:43,165 --> 00:26:44,534
- It gets dry and isn't tasty. - It doesn't taste good...

677
00:26:44,535 --> 00:26:45,604
- after some time. - It turns yellow too.

678
00:26:45,605 --> 00:26:46,605
Right.

679
00:26:46,606 --> 00:26:48,245
It's fun to stock up on cooked rice too.

680
00:26:48,945 --> 00:26:51,574
I transfer the rice into small containers on purpose.

681
00:26:51,575 --> 00:26:52,575
- I see. - Okay.

682
00:26:52,576 --> 00:26:54,845
- Why? - I don't eat that much rice.

683
00:26:55,545 --> 00:26:56,984
Isn't it because you want the rice to get warm faster?

684
00:26:56,985 --> 00:26:58,055
My gosh.

685
00:26:58,685 --> 00:27:01,224
It's so tiny. It looks smaller in person.

686
00:27:01,225 --> 00:27:02,785
(Surprised)

687
00:27:03,155 --> 00:27:06,125
(She washes the rice cooker after portioning the rice.)

688
00:27:07,355 --> 00:27:10,065
(She shakes off the water and finishes up.)

689
00:27:11,095 --> 00:27:12,865
(Tearing the kitchen towel)

690
00:27:14,805 --> 00:27:16,964
- What was that? - Hey.

691
00:27:16,965 --> 00:27:18,925
You knew you almost pulled out the faucet, right?

692
00:27:19,305 --> 00:27:20,835
(A sharp movement)

693
00:27:21,005 --> 00:27:22,375
I have to be quick.

694
00:27:23,105 --> 00:27:24,314
I'd run the water...

695
00:27:24,315 --> 00:27:25,544
- and adjusted my hand speed. - Right.

696
00:27:25,545 --> 00:27:26,614
- That's a waste of water. - I see.

697
00:27:26,615 --> 00:27:27,844
- But she adjusted the water speed. - That's wasteful.

698
00:27:27,845 --> 00:27:29,585
Right. I was dumb.

699
00:27:33,415 --> 00:27:35,885
(Quick)

700
00:27:36,155 --> 00:27:38,031
(She precisely gets a small amount of water on the kitchen towel.)

701
00:27:38,055 --> 00:27:39,124
I clean the lid right away.

702
00:27:39,125 --> 00:27:40,624
- The lid of the rice cooker. - It works better than wet wipes.

703
00:27:40,625 --> 00:27:42,665
I can tell that she has done this many times.

704
00:27:43,065 --> 00:27:44,895
You wipe the inside gently.

705
00:27:45,295 --> 00:27:48,205
It's easier to do this than have a major clean-out.

706
00:27:49,065 --> 00:27:51,385
(This time, she organizes the rice she has portioned out.)

707
00:27:52,235 --> 00:27:53,534
You really seem like a soldier.

708
00:27:53,535 --> 00:27:54,805
(Organized)

709
00:27:55,975 --> 00:27:57,005
All done.

710
00:28:01,245 --> 00:28:02,384
It's neat.

711
00:28:02,385 --> 00:28:03,445
All done.

712
00:28:04,655 --> 00:28:05,854
(She brushes her teeth to keep them clean.)

713
00:28:05,855 --> 00:28:07,085
I'm busy.

714
00:28:09,155 --> 00:28:11,555
That's tooth powder.

715
00:28:11,725 --> 00:28:13,224
- Tooth powder. - People use it these days.

716
00:28:13,225 --> 00:28:16,024
- It gets rid of plaques. - Mites?

717
00:28:16,025 --> 00:28:17,295
- Mites? - Not mites.

718
00:28:17,565 --> 00:28:18,734
- Hold on. - Why would there be mites?

719
00:28:18,735 --> 00:28:20,294
- Mites on the teeth? - I'm not the "Predator" monster.

720
00:28:20,295 --> 00:28:22,535
Why do you keep talking about mites?

721
00:28:23,905 --> 00:28:25,411
(He has a lot of interest in the mites.)

722
00:28:25,435 --> 00:28:28,144
(Gagging)

723
00:28:28,145 --> 00:28:29,375
That's it. It's refreshing.

724
00:28:29,445 --> 00:28:31,891
- I do it in a refreshing way. - You brush the nooks and crannies.

725
00:28:31,915 --> 00:28:33,235
You stick the toothbrush in deep.

726
00:28:33,545 --> 00:28:34,584
All right.

727
00:28:34,585 --> 00:28:36,545
(Feeling refreshed, she heads to the kitchen.)

728
00:28:38,385 --> 00:28:39,755
She is so diligent.

729
00:28:40,255 --> 00:28:41,285
Seriously.

730
00:28:42,885 --> 00:28:44,825
(Clattering)

731
00:28:50,865 --> 00:28:54,505
(She begins to vacuum.)

732
00:28:59,475 --> 00:29:02,905
(She vacuums under the rug.)

733
00:29:09,085 --> 00:29:10,661
- She put on a hat. - Is that for vacuuming?

734
00:29:10,685 --> 00:29:11,714
She looks hip.

735
00:29:11,715 --> 00:29:12,914
(She wears a hat so that her hair won't fall.)

736
00:29:12,915 --> 00:29:14,915
It isn't easy to look good in a short-brimmed hat.

737
00:29:15,225 --> 00:29:17,585
(Whirring)

738
00:29:19,095 --> 00:29:22,265
(She resumes vacuuming after locking her hair in a hat.)

739
00:29:27,535 --> 00:29:29,665
The troublesome chores must be done 1st or 2nd.

740
00:29:30,635 --> 00:29:33,175
It feels good when the house is clean.

741
00:29:33,735 --> 00:29:35,535
It's especially true when you have a day off.

742
00:29:36,575 --> 00:29:39,015
When trash is lying around,

743
00:29:39,445 --> 00:29:41,545
and the air is stuffy, it gets so tiring.

744
00:29:42,685 --> 00:29:44,044
What? How many are there?

745
00:29:44,045 --> 00:29:45,514
It's "Charlie and the Chocolate Factory."

746
00:29:45,515 --> 00:29:47,584
I also thought it was "Charlie and the Chocolate Factory."

747
00:29:47,585 --> 00:29:48,884
- Seriously. - It really seems that way...

748
00:29:48,885 --> 00:29:50,855
because she is wearing a hat.

749
00:29:52,195 --> 00:29:54,195
You know, it's really eerie.

750
00:29:54,765 --> 00:29:56,294
It's "Charlie and the Japchae Factory."

751
00:29:56,295 --> 00:29:57,324
- Japchae. - It has that vibe.

752
00:29:57,325 --> 00:29:58,694
- Glass noodle. - Glass noodle factory.

753
00:29:58,695 --> 00:29:59,935
- Glass noodle factory. - Yes.

754
00:30:01,105 --> 00:30:02,664
(The sanitation procedure is thorough...)

755
00:30:02,665 --> 00:30:04,505
(at "Charlie and the Japchae Factory.")

756
00:30:05,105 --> 00:30:07,135
(She vacuums every inch of the house.)

757
00:30:08,745 --> 00:30:09,805
All done.

758
00:30:10,875 --> 00:30:12,575
(She puts the vacuum back in its place.)

759
00:30:13,775 --> 00:30:15,685
(Plopping down)

760
00:30:16,445 --> 00:30:18,385
(Staring)

761
00:30:18,915 --> 00:30:20,055
It's 2:30pm.

762
00:30:20,715 --> 00:30:22,225
By 3pm...

763
00:30:23,155 --> 00:30:27,025
(She gets back up after checking the time.)

764
00:30:27,965 --> 00:30:30,125
(Opening)

765
00:30:32,435 --> 00:30:33,465
Let's see.

766
00:30:33,835 --> 00:30:35,981
- Oh, my. It's completely full. - There are so many shoes.

767
00:30:36,005 --> 00:30:37,204
I see a lot of shoes.

768
00:30:37,205 --> 00:30:38,904
It'd be impossible to know what kind of shoes you have.

769
00:30:38,905 --> 00:30:40,035
I know every single pair.

770
00:30:40,205 --> 00:30:42,175
(Let's see.)

771
00:30:43,445 --> 00:30:44,475
Here.

772
00:30:48,415 --> 00:30:50,114
- This one is full too. - This one has shoes too.

773
00:30:50,115 --> 00:30:51,754
(It's also full of shoes.)

774
00:30:51,755 --> 00:30:53,685
There are daily shoes...

775
00:30:54,555 --> 00:30:58,195
as well as shoes of different purposes.

776
00:30:58,625 --> 00:31:00,995
(Humming)

777
00:31:03,965 --> 00:31:05,495
I pick a pair in my head.

778
00:31:06,235 --> 00:31:07,594
- You pick a pair you'll wear. - Yes.

779
