(While cheonggukjang boils, )

1039
00:42:49,726 --> 00:42:51,895
(she rummages through the fridge again.)

1040
00:42:51,896 --> 00:42:53,266
Where did it go? What?

1041
00:42:56,166 --> 00:42:57,235
(Suddenly, an alarm goes off for 6:25pm.)

1042
00:42:57,236 --> 00:42:58,266
What is the alarm for?

1043
00:42:58,336 --> 00:42:59,982
- It's an important alarm. - Five minutes.

1044
00:43:00,006 --> 00:43:01,066
It's the end of her shift.

1045
00:43:03,876 --> 00:43:05,345
A-3.

1046
00:43:05,346 --> 00:43:07,376
- It's the end of her shift. - Come on in.

1047
00:43:08,446 --> 00:43:11,546
(Here comes A-3.)

1048
00:43:12,346 --> 00:43:15,686
(The tasks are divided at the glass noodle factory.)

1049
00:43:16,756 --> 00:43:18,856
(Pressing the remote)

1050
00:43:20,026 --> 00:43:21,156
Baseball?

1051
00:43:22,856 --> 00:43:24,425
The game was scheduled for 6:30pm.

1052
00:43:24,426 --> 00:43:25,696
That's right. The evening game.

1053
00:43:27,196 --> 00:43:29,135
(She set an alarm for a baseball match.)

1054
00:43:29,136 --> 00:43:30,296
You'll watch it when it airs.

1055
00:43:32,066 --> 00:43:34,536
(Kneeling)

1056
00:43:36,436 --> 00:43:39,045
(Please let my team win today.)

1057
00:43:39,046 --> 00:43:40,446
I prayed with devotion in my heart.

1058
00:43:41,046 --> 00:43:46,286
(She prays for victory with devotion in her heart.)

1059
00:43:48,016 --> 00:43:50,056
(She rushes over after praying.)

1060
00:43:51,056 --> 00:43:52,256
I think I'm ready.

1061
00:43:52,986 --> 00:43:54,926
- My goodness. - Gosh.

1062
00:43:55,226 --> 00:43:56,356
I need to wear it.

1063
00:43:56,596 --> 00:43:59,326
I need to wear the uniform before the game begins.

1064
00:44:00,296 --> 00:44:02,736
(With the baseball channel on, she heads back to the fridge.)

1065
00:44:03,796 --> 00:44:06,036
(Taking out)

1066
00:44:06,866 --> 00:44:10,535
(She takes out a familiar container with care.)

1067
00:44:10,536 --> 00:44:11,975
- Glass noodles? - I think so.

1068
00:44:11,976 --> 00:44:13,006
That's right.

1069
00:44:13,176 --> 00:44:14,456
She's addicted to glass noodles.

1070
00:44:16,316 --> 00:44:17,996
I grabbed only a bit. You saw that, right?

1071
00:44:18,576 --> 00:44:20,445
- Charlie and glass noodle factory. - I grabbed only a bit, right?

1072
00:44:20,446 --> 00:44:21,846
I feel bad for the glass noodles.

1073
00:44:22,016 --> 00:44:23,215
I grabbed only a bit.

1074
00:44:23,216 --> 00:44:25,092
But glass noodles don't go well with cheonggukjang.

1075
00:44:25,116 --> 00:44:26,555
Of course, they do.

1076
00:44:26,556 --> 00:44:28,025
- I've never seen that combination. - Me neither.

1077
00:44:28,026 --> 00:44:29,395
Do people add glass noodles to cheonggukjang?

1078
00:44:29,396 --> 00:44:30,425
- I've never seen it. - They're for...

1079
00:44:30,426 --> 00:44:31,455
seolleongtang and gomtang.

1080
00:44:31,456 --> 00:44:33,125
As you add thin noodles to seolleongtang,

1081
00:44:33,126 --> 00:44:34,495
you add glass noodles to cheonggukjang.

1082
00:44:34,496 --> 00:44:36,165
I guess she adds them whenever there's moisture.

1083
00:44:36,166 --> 00:44:37,612
(I guess she adds them whenever there's moisture.)

1084
00:44:37,636 --> 00:44:39,465
- No. - Moisture isn't a must.

1085
00:44:39,466 --> 00:44:40,565
She adds them even when there's no moisture.

1086
00:44:40,566 --> 00:44:42,206
Are you talking about an aquatic animal?

1087
00:44:42,506 --> 00:44:45,106
- Moisture, my foot. - Seriously.

1088
00:44:45,776 --> 00:44:47,806
I'll add a bit to cheonggukjang.

1089
00:44:49,016 --> 00:44:50,316
I only added a bit.

1090
00:44:51,716 --> 00:44:53,985
(She ate them in the morning.)

1091
00:44:53,986 --> 00:44:55,515
I ate a lot in the morning...

1092
00:44:55,516 --> 00:44:57,085
- because I needed to move. - You ate a lot in the morning.

1093
00:44:57,086 --> 00:44:58,455
I added a bit this time.

1094
00:44:58,456 --> 00:45:00,376
(She eats glass noodles in the evening as well.)

1095
00:45:00,686 --> 00:45:02,032
Tofu is tasty when you tear it like that.

1096
00:45:02,056 --> 00:45:03,902
- She tears it with her hands. - You do it with your hands.

1097
00:45:03,926 --> 00:45:05,465
- They absorb the soup well. - They look like soft tofu.

1098
00:45:05,466 --> 00:45:07,386
- They absorb the soup quickly. - That's a plus.

1099
00:45:08,126 --> 00:45:09,966
(The jjigae is ready.)

1100
00:45:11,596 --> 00:45:13,465
(She seasons the julienned radish as the glass noodles cook.)

1101
00:45:13,466 --> 00:45:15,586
- That's salted shrimp. Yes. - Is that salted shrimp?

1102
00:45:15,936 --> 00:45:17,406
Let's add a bit of rice syrup.

1103
00:45:20,006 --> 00:45:21,145
I don't add sugar.

1104
00:45:21,146 --> 00:45:23,076
- You don't add sugar. - I don't add sugar.

1105
00:45:26,216 --> 00:45:27,916
Let's add chili powder.

1106
00:45:28,286 --> 00:45:29,386
I add chili powder.

1107
00:45:30,956 --> 00:45:33,486
- I add sesame oil and garlic. - It looks delicious.

1108
00:45:33,686 --> 00:45:36,026
- It's bound to be tasty. - It looks amazing.

1109
00:45:37,756 --> 00:45:38,765
(Shin Young massages the ingredients with her hands.)

1110
00:45:38,766 --> 00:45:39,826
My goodness.

1111
00:45:40,526 --> 00:45:42,196
- It looks delicious. - Seriously.

1112
00:45:44,266 --> 00:45:47,206
(Humming)

1113
00:45:49,136 --> 00:45:52,376
- I'm humming by myself. - It's the definition of happiness.

1114
00:45:52,776 --> 00:45:54,575
The cooking process is nice.

1115
00:45:54,576 --> 00:45:56,416
- It's therapeutic to watch. - I know.

1116
00:45:58,076 --> 00:46:00,616
(Salivating)

1117
00:46:02,216 --> 00:46:04,055
(She tries a bit.)

1118
00:46:04,056 --> 00:46:05,315
It looks like she is stealing food.

1119
00:46:05,316 --> 00:46:06,356
(She tries a bit.)

1120
00:46:06,656 --> 00:46:07,856
It's tasty.

1121
00:46:09,196 --> 00:46:11,456
- It looks delicious. - The flavors fill the mouth.

1122
00:46:12,596 --> 00:46:13,996
(My goodness)

1123
00:46:15,496 --> 00:46:17,056
Should I star in "Culinary Class Wars?"

1124
00:46:18,496 --> 00:46:19,536
I'm sorry.

1125
00:46:19,666 --> 00:46:22,335
You'll make it to the team match. I'm serious.

1126
00:46:22,336 --> 00:46:24,306
- Are you serious? - Glass Noodle Addict.

1127
00:46:24,436 --> 00:46:25,605
Glass Noodle Addict!

1128
00:46:25,606 --> 00:46:27,846
(Glass Noodle Addict, Eats glass noodles for every meal)

1129
00:46:28,206 --> 00:46:29,316
It's so tasty.

1130
00:46:30,016 --> 00:46:31,175
Gosh, it's so tasty.

1131
00:46:31,176 --> 00:46:33,576
(She's absorbed in the taste, but she still cleans up well.)

1132
00:46:34,886 --> 00:46:37,686
(She washes the dirty dishes right away.)

1133
00:46:38,716 --> 00:46:40,516
I don't feel lazy when I clean up right away.

1134
00:46:43,596 --> 00:46:44,755
(She puts away the condiments as well.)

1135
00:46:44,756 --> 00:46:46,725
I won't have to look for them when I put things back.

1136
00:46:46,726 --> 00:46:48,026
- Right. - Since I know that,

1137
00:46:48,066 --> 00:46:49,835
I keep thinking about the future.

1138
00:46:49,836 --> 00:46:50,865
Right.

1139
00:46:50,866 --> 00:46:52,235
I think that is why things are kept clean...

1140
00:46:52,236 --> 00:46:54,136
- even when there are many things. - Right.

1141
00:46:55,406 --> 00:46:56,436
(Peeking)

1142
00:46:56,836 --> 00:46:58,035
(It's already 7pm.)

1143
00:46:58,036 --> 00:46:59,076
Okay.

1144
00:47:00,246 --> 00:47:01,575
(Opening)

1145
00:47:01,576 --> 00:47:03,522
How many times are you opening the fridge in a single day?

1146
00:47:03,546 --> 00:47:05,816
I didn't open it that much.

1147
00:47:07,786 --> 00:47:10,356
(She takes out the frozen rice.)

1148
00:47:11,416 --> 00:47:12,486
It's too small.

1149
00:47:13,186 --> 00:47:14,956
(Torn)

1150
00:47:15,286 --> 00:47:17,226
It does look really small.

1151
00:47:19,166 --> 00:47:21,595
(They get put into the microwave after a lot of thought.)

1152
00:47:21,596 --> 00:47:22,866
But two containers were put in.

1153
00:47:24,836 --> 00:47:26,465
- Right. - Did you see that?

1154
00:47:26,466 --> 00:47:27,466
(Did you see that?)

1155
00:47:29,236 --> 00:47:30,705
The containers did look really small.

1156
00:47:30,706 --> 00:47:32,305
- Come on! - I expected two containers.

1157
00:47:32,306 --> 00:47:33,306
- Right. - Right.

1158
00:47:34,646 --> 00:47:35,945
(She sets the table with the food she made after that.)

1159
00:47:35,946 --> 00:47:37,616
I had to set the table with great food.

1160
00:47:38,446 --> 00:47:40,615
(Even the hot cheonggukjang gets moved.)

1161
00:47:40,616 --> 00:47:41,716
Gosh, that was darn hot.

1162
00:47:45,626 --> 00:47:48,756
(She takes out the heated up rice containers with both hands.)

1163
00:47:49,856 --> 00:47:53,226
(She places everything together nicely.)

1164
00:47:54,566 --> 00:47:55,935
- Gosh! - Gosh, it's cheonggukjang.

1165
00:47:55,936 --> 00:47:58,266
(Radish cheonggukjang and spicy radish)

1166
00:47:59,636 --> 00:48:01,836
- Cheongdo's water parsley. - Cheongdo's water parsley.

1167
00:48:02,636 --> 00:48:04,435
(The food for watching baseball at home is ready.)

1168
00:48:04,436 --> 00:48:07,906
I prefer a home meal like this instead of chicken and beer.

1169
00:48:08,046 --> 00:48:10,176
It feels like the cafeteria of the players.

1170
00:48:12,486 --> 00:48:14,146
(Moving)

1171
00:48:15,546 --> 00:48:17,616
(The TV is moved as well.)

1172
00:48:19,356 --> 00:48:21,685
- Gosh, it's the perfect setup... - Let's watch it now.

1173
00:48:21,686 --> 00:48:22,846
- to watch baseball. - Right.

1174
00:48:23,826 --> 00:48:26,865
(During breakfast or dinner...)

1175
00:48:26,866 --> 00:48:28,396
Thank you for the meal.

1176
00:48:28,526 --> 00:48:31,046
Even the mothers of the players don't watch this much baseball.

1177
00:48:31,396 --> 00:48:33,905
This is a treat that can be enjoyed for being diligent.

1178
00:48:33,906 --> 00:48:35,036
- Right. - It really is.

1179
00:48:35,636 --> 00:48:37,376
(She takes a bite of the stew first.)

1180
00:48:42,646 --> 00:48:43,646
(Letting out the steam)

1181
00:48:45,046 --> 00:48:46,446
(Satisfied)

1182
00:48:47,386 --> 00:48:50,385
It was so hot. It felt like my the roof of my mouth...

1183
00:48:50,386 --> 00:48:52,755
was going to get burned by the tofu's warmth.

1184
00:48:52,756 --> 00:48:54,676
You'll have to tear out the blisters afterwards.

1185
00:48:55,256 --> 00:48:56,996
(Hold on.)

1186
00:48:57,596 --> 00:48:59,666
(She picks up the glass noodles first.)

1187
00:48:59,996 --> 00:49:02,266
Like an aperitif, I started with the glass noodles.

1188
00:49:02,566 --> 00:49:03,666
An aperitif?

1189
00:49:04,836 --> 00:49:07,466
(Just like in the morning)

1190
00:49:07,536 --> 00:49:09,976
I'm curious how glass noodles would taste with cheonggukjang.

1191
00:49:11,176 --> 00:49:12,206
Gosh!

1192
00:49:12,446 --> 00:49:14,522
- That tastes so good. - You're addicted to glass noodles.

1193
00:49:14,546 --> 00:49:16,576
(Satisfied)

1194
00:49:19,516 --> 00:49:20,745
(Focused)

1195
00:49:20,746 --> 00:49:21,816
Unlike what I thought,

1196
00:49:22,556 --> 00:49:25,686
she's not watching much of the baseball.

1197
00:49:26,926 --> 00:49:28,225
- I was listening. - Right.

1198
00:49:28,226 --> 00:49:29,655
- I was listening to everything... - Baseball fans...

1199
00:49:29,656 --> 00:49:31,072
- that was happening. - listen to what's going on.

1200
00:49:31,096 --> 00:49:33,895
- You don't watch it with your eyes. - I'm aware of everything.

1201
00:49:33,896 --> 00:49:36,296
Even still, you really aren't watching the screen that much.

1202
00:49:37,196 --> 00:49:39,436
This is when she watches the screen.

1203
00:49:39,806 --> 00:49:42,506
It's when she turns her head to lift the chopsticks.

1204
00:49:43,236 --> 00:49:44,405
When she cools off the food that's hot.

1205
00:49:44,406 --> 00:49:46,805
- When the food is hot. - Right.

1206
00:49:46,806 --> 00:49:49,345
Just at that moment when she puts in the food.

1207
00:49:49,346 --> 00:49:50,445
After blowing on the food.

1208
00:49:50,446 --> 00:49:52,326
Blowing on the food and coming back down again.

1209
00:49:52,816 --> 00:49:54,645
(She picks up the hot glass noodles.)

1210
00:49:54,646 --> 00:49:57,115
I watched it for a brief moment.

1211
00:49:57,116 --> 00:49:59,126
- You watched it. - I watched it, didn't I?

1212
00:49:59,656 --> 00:50:02,726
(Her eyes are stuck on the food unless she's cooling it.)

1213
00:50:03,126 --> 00:50:05,246
I should've added in a bit more of the glass noodles.

1214
00:50:07,326 --> 00:50:10,596
(Disappointed)

1215
00:50:12,366 --> 00:50:15,936
(Slurping)

1216
00:50:16,976 --> 00:50:19,406
(She puts down the plate of glass noodles she's done with.)

1217
00:50:19,976 --> 00:50:21,906
All right. This is the second course now.

1218
00:50:22,416 --> 00:50:23,416
White rice.

1219
00:50:23,916 --> 00:50:25,175
My favorite white rice.

1220
00:50:25,176 --> 00:50:27,846
(She starts her meal in earnest with white rice.)

1221
00:50:28,486 --> 00:50:30,216
(Scooping)

1222
00:50:31,116 --> 00:50:34,156
(She wets the rice with the soup.)

1223
00:50:35,756 --> 00:50:36,826
It will go in here.

1224
00:50:38,026 --> 00:50:40,466
- I mixed it together. - Gosh, that looks good.

1225
00:50:40,966 --> 00:50:42,736
It's a small container of rice.

1226
00:50:43,066 --> 00:50:44,395
The cheonggukjang looks really good.

1227
00:50:44,396 --> 00:50:45,465
(She takes a bite after diligently mixing it.)

1228
00:50:45,466 --> 00:50:48,306
(Yelling)

1229
00:50:49,276 --> 00:50:50,306
(Disappointed)

1230
00:50:51,636 --> 00:50:54,405
(She clears up a bowl of rice as if nothing happened.)

1231
00:50:54,406 --> 00:50:56,146
(Getting up)

1232
00:50:57,576 --> 00:50:58,745
(The container she finished with gets put into the sink right away.)

1233
00:50:58,746 --> 00:51:00,915
- While you are eating? - She went right away.

1234
00:51:00,916 --> 00:51:02,656
I just rinsed it with water.

1235
00:51:05,316 --> 00:51:08,656
Shall I start my meal in earnest?

1236
00:51:09,326 --> 00:51:11,526
- What? - Was there something else?

1237
00:51:12,601 --> 00:51:13,831
Shall I start my meal...

1238
00:51:14,331 --> 00:51:15,600
- What? - in earnest?

1239
00:51:15,601 --> 00:51:17,930
- Was there something else? - So there are different courses.

1240
00:51:17,931 --> 00:51:19,040
There are different courses.

1241
00:51:19,041 --> 00:51:20,541
- Right. - Courses 1, 2, 3, 4.

1242
00:51:20,701 --> 00:51:21,701
I can't help it.

1243
00:51:21,702 --> 00:51:24,271
(Rice gets put into a brass bowl.)

1244
00:51:25,681 --> 00:51:26,740
- Why was the spicy radish there? - Gosh!

1245
00:51:26,741 --> 00:51:28,257
- It's for that reason! - That looks so good!

1246
00:51:28,281 --> 00:51:30,350
- Gosh, it's making me drool. - Spicy radish is love.

1247
00:51:30,351 --> 00:51:31,651
That looks so good.

1248
00:51:33,521 --> 00:51:35,851
The rice containers were tiny.

1249
00:51:36,351 --> 00:51:39,161
It was like bird feed. The rice containers were this small.

1250
00:51:39,191 --> 00:51:41,871
- What kind of bird eats that much? - I put in one whole container.

1251
00:51:41,931 --> 00:51:45,261
I had an original meal with one small container.

1252
00:51:45,501 --> 00:51:49,531
I put in some spicy radish in a brass bowl.

1253
00:51:49,731 --> 00:51:53,100
People love food made by their mom or dad.

1254
00:51:53,101 --> 00:51:55,940
But my food is the best to me.

1255
00:51:55,941 --> 00:51:57,011
It's amazing.

1256
00:51:57,971 --> 00:51:59,741
What an abundant life.

1257
00:52:00,481 --> 00:52:02,081
Stop being on a diet, everyone.

1258
00:52:03,181 --> 00:52:04,511
She's showing us how it's done.

1259
00:52:05,581 --> 00:52:06,881
Nice.

1260
00:52:07,551 --> 00:52:08,921
I'll add in some cheonggukjang.

1261
00:52:09,691 --> 00:52:11,521
The cheonggukjang is added.

1262
00:52:12,091 --> 00:52:13,660
Gosh, that looks so good.

1263
00:52:13,661 --> 00:52:15,731
- Gosh. - Gosh, that looks amazing.

1264
00:52:15,891 --> 00:52:16,931
Gosh.

1265
00:52:24,801 --> 00:52:25,841
Gosh!

1266
00:52:29,541 --> 00:52:31,071
(Picking up)

1267
00:52:32,111 --> 00:52:35,411
(She places the water parsley neatly.)

1268
00:52:37,381 --> 00:52:38,480
(She places rice on top of the water parsley wrap.)

1269
00:52:38,481 --> 00:52:40,681
So that's made into a wrap.

1270
00:52:41,821 --> 00:52:44,621
(She takes a great big bite.)

1271
00:52:46,421 --> 00:52:47,990
(Touched)

1272
00:52:47,991 --> 00:52:49,891
Try eating it like that, everyone.

1273
00:52:50,431 --> 00:52:51,907
- As a wrap like that? - With some water parsley.

1274
00:52:51,931 --> 00:52:53,430
- Right. Twirl up the water parsley. - Hey, Shin Young.

1275
00:52:53,431 --> 00:52:55,031
You're not watching the game at all!

1276
00:52:55,571 --> 00:52:56,870
I watched it a while ago.

1277
00:52:56,871 --> 00:52:58,777
- I watched it a while ago. - Who is batting right now?

1278
00:52:58,801 --> 00:53:00,941
- The Lions. - The jersey feels like an apron.

1279
00:53:01,441 --> 00:53:02,841
- Right. - In case she spills food.

1280
00:53:02,911 --> 00:53:04,340
- I watched it. - An apron?

1281
00:53:04,341 --> 00:53:05,480
- It will be washed afterwards. - Right.

1282
00:53:05,481 --> 00:53:07,541
That spoon appeared the most today.

1283
00:53:07,741 --> 00:53:09,050
(Bulgogi, seaweed soup, cheonggukjang, and other dishes)

1284
00:53:09,051 --> 00:53:11,011
- Right. - That is all I can remember.

1285
00:53:11,111 --> 00:53:13,320
That spoon is quite spacious.

1286
00:53:13,321 --> 00:53:14,681
I love how it's wide and deep.

1287
00:53:16,521 --> 00:53:17,620
(She's focusing on the spoon instead of the baseball game.)

1288
00:53:17,621 --> 00:53:18,820
Gosh, it's so good.

1289
00:53:18,821 --> 00:53:21,181
(She's focusing on the spoon instead of the baseball game.)

1290
00:53:22,531 --> 00:53:23,561
(Slurping)

1291
00:53:24,391 --> 00:53:25,401
(Slurping)

1292
00:53:27,061 --> 00:53:29,900
(Without thinking about the baseball game, )

1293
00:53:29,901 --> 00:53:32,741
(she's only focusing on her meal.)

1294
00:53:35,001 --> 00:53:36,371
Gosh, there's no more.

1295
00:53:37,111 --> 00:53:39,241
- She's disappointed. - I should control myself.

1296
00:53:40,681 --> 00:53:45,051
(She scrapes until the last grain of rice.)

1297
00:53:45,821 --> 00:53:47,650
Gosh, she finished all of that.

1298
00:53:47,651 --> 00:53:49,691
I was controlling myself. It was a controlled meal.

1299
00:53:52,191 --> 00:53:53,390
Gosh, that was great.

1300
00:53:53,391 --> 00:53:55,511
(She ends her dinner meal while controlling herself.)

1301
00:53:55,631 --> 00:53:57,231
Gosh, that was a great meal.

1302
00:53:59,431 --> 00:54:02,931
(The used dishes get put into the sink.)

1303
00:54:04,771 --> 00:54:06,801
Gosh, that was a happy meal.

1304
00:54:06,901 --> 00:54:08,400
I felt so satisfied.

1305
00:54:08,401 --> 00:54:10,711
- You looked really happy. - You looked really happy.

1306
00:54:12,181 --> 00:54:13,301
She's watching baseball now.

1307
00:54:13,741 --> 00:54:14,981
I turned it around.

1308
00:54:16,151 --> 00:54:18,481
(She heads to the sink after turning the TV.)

1309
00:54:19,351 --> 00:54:21,650
- While doing the dishes? - Right. While doing the dishes.

1310
00:54:21,651 --> 00:54:23,091
Gosh, that's the best.

1311
00:54:24,091 --> 00:54:27,021
Nice. The bases are loaded.

1312
00:54:28,061 --> 00:54:29,691
(Her hands are quick...)

1313
00:54:29,831 --> 00:54:31,671
(and her eyes are fixed on the baseball game.)

1314
00:54:32,161 --> 00:54:34,701
(She cleans up while being entertained.)

1315
00:54:34,931 --> 00:54:36,031
Done!

1316
00:54:38,001 --> 00:54:40,941
(She hurries over to the living room.)

1317
00:54:41,501 --> 00:54:44,141
(I must carry on watching the baseball game.)

1318
00:54:45,141 --> 00:54:47,381
(Grabbing)

1319
00:54:48,441 --> 00:54:51,951
(She gets some rest after getting everything done.)

1320
00:54:52,551 --> 00:54:53,551
Gosh, my back.

1321
00:54:56,221 --> 00:54:57,750
(Heave-ho!)

1322
00:54:57,751 --> 00:54:59,120
She's feeling guilty!

1323
00:54:59,121 --> 00:55:00,720
(Embarrassed)

1324
00:55:00,721 --> 00:55:01,721
Right!

1325
00:55:04,461 --> 00:55:06,201
Okay.

1326
00:55:07,831 --> 00:55:08,871
Nice.

1327
00:55:10,271 --> 00:55:11,900
We have to score with this chance.

1328
00:55:11,901 --> 00:55:14,021
(Shin Young lies down for the first time in 8 hours.)

1329
00:55:14,401 --> 00:55:16,541
(Getting up)

1330
00:55:18,981 --> 00:55:20,381
- What is that? - What is that?

1331
00:55:22,211 --> 00:55:23,651
There were sweet potato chips.

1332
00:55:23,811 --> 00:55:24,951
Sweet potato chips?

1333
00:55:25,481 --> 00:55:27,721
I've never seen sweet potato chips kept in a couch.

1334
00:55:29,921 --> 00:55:32,720
It has cabinets. I made the most of them.

1335
00:55:32,721 --> 00:55:36,230
Sweet potato chips on one side, and cheese chips on the other.

1336
00:55:36,231 --> 00:55:38,060
- Everything is in place for you. - That's right.

1337
00:55:38,061 --> 00:55:40,661
Also, there are trash cans on both sides.

1338
00:55:41,261 --> 00:55:42,800
- Gosh. - When I'm lying on this side,

1339
00:55:42,801 --> 00:55:44,000
I can throw it away on this side.

1340
00:55:44,001 --> 00:55:45,947
When I'm lying on that side, I can throw it away on that side.

1341
00:55:45,971 --> 00:55:48,200
- This scene is pure happiness. - Right.

1342
00:55:48,201 --> 00:55:49,871
(Pure happiness)

1343
00:55:53,411 --> 00:55:55,311
(Sliding in)

1344
00:55:55,711 --> 00:55:56,780
- What are you doing? - It must be something good.

1345
00:55:56,781 --> 00:55:57,950
- Why are you eating so secretly? - Why are you eating like that?

1346
00:55:57,951 --> 00:55:59,581
What? Why are you eating like that?

1347
00:56:01,781 --> 00:56:02,820
The crumbs!

1348
00:56:02,821 --> 00:56:04,190
- In case the crumbs fall. - It's to hold the crumbs.

1349
00:56:04,191 --> 00:56:06,760
- In case the crumbs fall. - You eat like an otter.

1350
00:56:06,761 --> 00:56:09,431
(You eat like an otter.)

1351
00:56:12,601 --> 00:56:13,961
I should sit up.

1352
00:56:14,531 --> 00:56:15,531
Safe!

1353
00:56:16,831 --> 00:56:17,831
(Screaming)

1354
00:56:18,231 --> 00:56:20,141
It's here!

1355
00:56:20,841 --> 00:56:21,901
Nice!

1356
00:56:23,171 --> 00:56:24,241
Nice!

1357
00:56:25,441 --> 00:56:27,141
(Well done!)

1358
00:56:28,681 --> 00:56:30,810
(Satisfied)

1359
00:56:30,811 --> 00:56:31,851
That's amazing.

1360
00:56:32,721 --> 00:56:34,151
I feel the happiest right now.

1361
00:56:34,651 --> 00:56:36,721
I love today the most, and I enjoy today the most.

1362
00:56:37,321 --> 00:56:39,861
When tomorrow comes, I will feel happy tomorrow.

1363
00:56:40,821 --> 00:56:43,391
My house is my own world. My country.

1364
00:56:43,931 --> 00:56:45,207
It's a place where I feel comfortable.

1365
00:56:45,231 --> 00:56:47,260
I can feel like I'm in a different country.

1366
00:56:47,261 --> 00:56:49,501
It's like my own globe.

1367
00:56:49,901 --> 00:56:51,201
A new world unfolds before me.

1368
00:56:51,571 --> 00:56:53,301
I feel happy that I'm safe and sound.

1369
00:56:53,871 --> 00:56:55,471
I can eat...

1370
00:56:55,841 --> 00:56:57,540
and do the things I want to do.

1371
00:56:57,541 --> 00:56:59,841
(I can eat and do the things I want to do.)

1372
00:57:00,141 --> 00:57:01,780
I started feeling very grateful at one point.

1373
00:57:01,781 --> 00:57:03,681
I used to be really stingy with myself.

1374
00:57:04,451 --> 00:57:06,750
"Gosh, this tastes so good!"

1375
00:57:06,751 --> 00:57:08,451
I wasn't able to say these things.

1376
00:57:09,091 --> 00:57:10,120
But this thought came into mind.

1377
00:57:10,121 --> 00:57:12,760
"Instead of being acknowledged by others,"

1378
00:57:12,761 --> 00:57:16,031
"wouldn't it be better for me to acknowledge myself?"

1379
00:57:16,291 --> 00:57:19,831
I think it's only a very thin line...

1380
00:57:20,161 --> 00:57:23,871
between a tragedy and a comedy in people's minds.

1381
00:57:25,701 --> 00:57:27,671
Gosh, what a game!

1382
00:57:29,811 --> 00:57:32,781
Nice! That was perfect.

1383
00:57:33,781 --> 00:57:34,881
That was great.

1384
00:57:36,551 --> 00:57:38,281
How did it feel to watch your life?

1385
00:57:38,581 --> 00:57:41,050
I feel a lot more at ease now.

1386
00:57:41,051 --> 00:57:42,390
- Right. - It feels comfortable...

1387
00:57:42,391 --> 00:57:43,620
to lay everything down.

1388
00:57:43,621 --> 00:57:45,421
I want to make the most of my life even more.

1389
00:57:46,021 --> 00:57:47,620
There are so many fun things in life.

1390
00:57:47,621 --> 00:57:51,390
I've been wondering why I've been...

1391
00:57:51,391 --> 00:57:53,501
wasting my time at home in the past.

1392
00:57:53,631 --> 00:57:56,431
- After going through those times, - Right.

1393
00:57:57,001 --> 00:57:58,571
I think she has learned...

1394
00:57:59,001 --> 00:58:01,701
how to become a friend for herself.

1395
00:58:02,571 --> 00:58:03,671
Right.

1396
00:58:04,411 --> 00:58:06,181
This was what I was really impressed with.

1397
00:58:06,311 --> 00:58:07,780
She decided to go on a diet...

1398
00:58:07,781 --> 00:58:09,980
- and maintained that for 13 years. - Right.

1399
00:58:09,981 --> 00:58:12,950
When she gets hooked on something, she makes the most out of it.

1400
00:58:12,951 --> 00:58:14,220
- She makes the most out of it. - Right.

1401
00:58:14,221 --> 00:58:16,267
- Right. That's what she's like. - That's hard to do.

1402
00:58:16,291 --> 00:58:17,897
- Right. - I have to reach the end of it.

1403
00:58:17,921 --> 00:58:19,590
- You're so impressive. - I want to copy you...

1404
00:58:19,591 --> 00:58:21,167
- and do the things you did. - That would be great.

1405
00:58:21,191 --> 00:58:23,830
Shin Young looks much brighter and happier.

1406
00:58:23,831 --> 00:58:25,160
We'll continue to root for you. Thank you!

1407
00:58:25,161 --> 00:58:26,301
Thank you!

1408
00:58:26,761 --> 00:58:27,931
(I Live Alone)

1409
00:58:34,041 --> 00:58:35,540
(Busy Day of Cooking)

1410
00:58:35,541 --> 00:58:36,541
"Busy Day of Cooking?"

1411
00:58:37,441 --> 00:58:40,381
(A crowded place)

1412
00:58:40,611 --> 00:58:41,740
(It's Hongdae, the street of youth.)

1413
00:58:41,741 --> 00:58:42,951
- What? - Gosh.

1414
00:58:43,111 --> 00:58:44,910
- You went there? - Hongdae?

1415
00:58:44,911 --> 00:58:46,380
Well, it's close to where I live.

1416
00:58:46,381 --> 00:58:49,142
(Woo Il heads somewhere with a bag that is as big as his upper body.)

1417
00:58:50,051 --> 00:58:52,291
- Hello! - Hello!

1418
00:58:52,921 --> 00:58:54,421
Hello. Drive safely.

1419
00:58:54,791 --> 00:58:55,991
(Chuckling)

1420
00:58:56,291 --> 00:58:58,330
- Hello. - Hello.

1421
00:58:58,331 --> 00:59:00,560
(He greets another person.)

1422
00:59:00,561 --> 00:59:04,601
I think it has been around 13 years since I lived in this area.

1423
00:59:04,801 --> 00:59:07,900
I see a lot of young university students.

1424
00:59:07,901 --> 00:59:10,571
Shall I say it energizes me?

1425
00:59:10,841 --> 00:59:12,481
Right. There's a certain vibe in Hongdae.

1426
00:59:17,381 --> 00:59:19,350
(Gosh, it's Mr. Nice.)

1427
00:59:19,351 --> 00:59:20,780
They're taking selfies. What's wrong with you?

1428
00:59:20,781 --> 00:59:21,821
Gosh.

1429
00:59:23,191 --> 00:59:24,451
At the crosswalk?

1430
00:59:25,151 --> 00:59:27,191
They're taking selfies. What are you doing?

1431
00:59:28,361 --> 00:59:31,131
(After showing his fan service that wasn't wanted, )

1432
00:59:31,931 --> 00:59:37,471
(he heads somewhere.)

1433
00:59:40,071 --> 00:59:42,341
(He walks in naturally.)

1434
00:59:42,841 --> 00:59:43,940
Hello.

1435
00:59:43,941 --> 00:59:45,610
- Hello. - Hello.

1436
00:59:45,611 --> 00:59:47,840
(He greets everyone naturally.)

1437
00:59:47,841 --> 00:59:49,350
- Hello. - Hello.

1438
00:59:49,351 --> 00:59:50,781
- Hello. - Hello.

1439
00:59:51,281 --> 00:59:52,350
- What? - Hello.

1440
00:59:52,351 --> 00:59:53,481
It's a kitchen.

1441
00:59:54,751 --> 00:59:58,751
This is the cooking school which I attend...

1442
00:59:59,091 --> 01:00:00,321
twice a week these days.

1443
01:00:01,861 --> 01:00:03,431
- You attend a cooking school? - Yes.

1444
01:00:03,761 --> 01:00:08,131
I wanted to start something at the start of this year.

1445
01:00:08,231 --> 01:00:09,951
One of the things I considered was cooking.

1446
01:00:10,331 --> 01:00:11,870
I realized that...

1447
01:00:11,871 --> 01:00:13,700
I didn't know how to cook anything.

1448
01:00:13,701 --> 01:00:16,271
While working these days,

1449
01:00:16,871 --> 01:00:19,841
cooking felt like something I couldn't leave out.

1450
01:00:20,011 --> 01:00:21,810
"Do you know how to cook something, Woo Il?"

1451
01:00:21,811 --> 01:00:23,580
"Are you good at cooking?"

1452
01:00:23,581 --> 01:00:25,981
I got asked these questions really often.

1453
01:00:26,081 --> 01:00:27,621
When I said I couldn't cook,

1454
01:00:28,151 --> 01:00:30,051
I didn't get any calls after that.

1455
01:00:30,591 --> 01:00:33,361
This happened quite a few times to me.

1456
01:00:33,921 --> 01:00:35,220
There's a certain feeling...

1457
01:00:35,221 --> 01:00:37,131
- that you might be a good cook. - Right.

1458
01:00:37,731 --> 01:00:42,260
That made me think that I should know how to...

1459
01:00:42,261 --> 01:00:43,430
cook a few dishes.

1460
01:00:43,431 --> 01:00:45,307
It was something that could be related to my work as well.

1461
01:00:45,331 --> 01:00:47,271
It has been a month since I attended.

1462
01:00:47,771 --> 01:00:49,171
I go twice a week.

1463
01:00:50,011 --> 01:00:51,870
I'm there for around four hours during each session.

1464
01:00:51,871 --> 01:00:54,031
(He's working hard to learn twice a week for 4 hours.)

1465
01:00:55,641 --> 01:00:57,880
(He's passionate enough to burn up the frying pan.)

1466
01:00:57,881 --> 01:00:59,421
You have to be careful with your hair.

1467
01:01:00,721 --> 01:01:03,391
(Woo Il is burning with passion for cooking.)

1468
01:01:04,021 --> 01:01:05,490
It was my first time applying for classes...

1469
01:01:05,491 --> 01:01:07,571
after attending cram school when I was 20 years old.

1470
01:01:08,891 --> 01:01:10,130
There was a touching moment.

1471
01:01:10,131 --> 01:01:12,600
It was my mother's birthday on the day I had class.

1472
01:01:12,601 --> 01:01:16,101
I gave my mother the food I made...

1473
01:01:16,771 --> 01:01:19,071
and she felt very touched.

1474
01:01:19,801 --> 01:01:20,871
- Gosh. - Gosh.

1475
01:01:20,901 --> 01:01:22,170
(Good son Woo Il's perspective)

1476
01:01:22,171 --> 01:01:23,240
This was what my mom said.

1477
01:01:23,241 --> 01:01:25,840
"I get to experience a day like this after living a long life."

1478
01:01:25,841 --> 01:01:29,551
"I didn't expect to eat the food you made."

1479
01:01:30,311 --> 01:01:32,981
That felt...

1480
01:01:33,751 --> 01:01:35,481
quite strange.

1481
01:01:36,351 --> 01:01:38,290
"You just need to get married now." Did she not say that afterward?

1482
01:01:38,291 --> 01:01:39,421
She did say that.

1483
01:01:40,391 --> 01:01:41,590
- Of course, she did. - Really?

1484
01:01:41,591 --> 01:01:43,861
"What's the point of making this for yourself?"

1485
01:01:44,691 --> 01:01:45,851
She kept saying those things.

1486
01:01:46,401 --> 01:01:47,560
"This is so much food."

1487
01:01:47,561 --> 01:01:49,001
"You'll end up throwing it away."

1488
01:01:49,371 --> 01:01:51,401
"If you cook for yourself,"

1489
01:01:51,871 --> 01:01:53,170
"you'll throw out half of it."

1490
01:01:53,171 --> 01:01:54,717
"You should know not to waste ingredients."

1491
01:01:54,741 --> 01:01:56,441
"You must have your own family."

1492
01:01:56,911 --> 01:01:59,480
"You must feel so lonely to eat this on your own."

1493
01:01:59,481 --> 01:02:00,811
It sparked a fire.

1494
01:02:03,211 --> 01:02:05,010
(He ties up his hair before class begins.)

1495
01:02:05,011 --> 01:02:06,020
- That's important. - Of course.

1496
01:02:06,021 --> 01:02:07,781
- Hygiene is the most important. - Right.

1497
01:02:08,581 --> 01:02:10,921
I got some drinks for everyone.

1498
01:02:11,151 --> 01:02:13,321
This is for everyone.

1499
01:02:13,991 --> 01:02:15,930
- I got drinks for everyone. - The students?

1500
01:02:15,931 --> 01:02:17,261
For the students as well.

1501
01:02:17,531 --> 01:02:20,731
I got you juice just in case you don't like coffee.

1502
01:02:21,931 --> 01:02:23,811
- Thank you. - This was the most expensive one.

1503
01:02:24,071 --> 01:02:25,430
This was the most expensive one.

1504
01:02:25,431 --> 01:02:28,241
Our class is a hobby class.

1505
01:02:28,271 --> 01:02:29,440
It's focused on home meals.

1506
01:02:29,441 --> 01:02:32,881
We learn simple dishes that can be made at home...

1507
01:02:33,041 --> 01:02:35,411
during our sessions.

1508
01:02:35,781 --> 01:02:39,720
As for the age group, there are really young students...

1509
01:02:39,721 --> 01:02:41,451
and seniors as well.

1510
01:02:41,651 --> 01:02:43,790
I didn't know there were so many people...

1511
01:02:43,791 --> 01:02:45,431
who wanted to learn how to cook in Korea.

1512
01:02:45,891 --> 01:02:48,021
That's great. It's a comfortable setting as well.

1513
01:02:48,521 --> 01:02:49,760
There are many people who want to learn how to cook.

1514
01:02:49,761 --> 01:02:53,031
(After sharing drinks with everyone, )

1515
01:02:54,301 --> 01:02:57,671
(Woo Il gets something out of the bag he brought.)

1516
01:02:59,901 --> 01:03:01,670
It has already begun.

1517
01:03:01,671 --> 01:03:03,070
- It has already begun... - Right.

1518
01:03:03,071 --> 01:03:04,071
With the preparations.

1519
01:03:05,411 --> 01:03:08,010
(He sets his station naturally.)

1520
01:03:08,011 --> 01:03:09,180
What number was it for that class?

1521
01:03:09,181 --> 01:03:11,380
That was my ninth class.

1522
01:03:11,381 --> 01:03:12,721
(It's already his 9th class.)

1523
01:03:13,951 --> 01:03:15,420
If you're ready, come to the front...

1524
01:03:15,421 --> 01:03:17,020
- with the recipe. - Right.

1525
01:03:17,021 --> 01:03:18,751
Come to the front.

1526
01:03:19,721 --> 01:03:20,861
All right. Today,

1527
01:03:21,521 --> 01:03:23,590
- we'll make the Andong jjimdak. - Jjimdak?

1528
01:03:23,591 --> 01:03:25,391
They'll be making jjimdak?

1529
01:03:25,831 --> 01:03:27,031
Steamed mussels and rice cake?

1530
01:03:27,061 --> 01:03:29,671
We'll make the rolled omelet as well as we will have enough time.

1531
01:03:29,771 --> 01:03:31,971
This class was quite difficult.

1532
01:03:32,331 --> 01:03:33,400
- Right? - Right. The chicken...

1533
01:03:33,401 --> 01:03:34,441
has to be prepared.

1534
01:03:34,901 --> 01:03:36,171
Right. She knows.

1535
01:03:36,371 --> 01:03:39,211
We'll start with the Andong jjimdak.

1536
01:03:39,281 --> 01:03:41,510
You each have been given a whole chicken.

1537
01:03:41,511 --> 01:03:44,550
You must make the cuts and remove the innards.

1538
01:03:44,551 --> 01:03:45,650
Potatoes, carrots, and onions.

1539
01:03:45,651 --> 01:03:48,980
We'll use sweet potatoes as well. Cut them into big pieces.

1540
01:03:48,981 --> 01:03:50,390
The order of the vegetables that are put in...

1541
01:03:50,391 --> 01:03:52,691
starts with the ones that need some time to get cooked.

1542
01:03:52,921 --> 01:03:55,761
I won't remove the skin for the sweet potatoes.

1543
01:03:55,921 --> 01:03:57,660
The sweet potatoes get mushy when they get cooked,

1544
01:03:57,661 --> 01:03:59,530
so put them in with the skin.

1545
01:03:59,531 --> 01:04:02,971
They will be used as big chunks.

1546
01:04:03,801 --> 01:04:05,771
- Around this size. - Why is the skin kept?

1547
01:04:06,171 --> 01:04:07,871
The jjimdak gets boiled for a long time.

1548
01:04:08,101 --> 01:04:10,382
That's to prevent the sweet potatoes from becoming mushy.

1549
01:04:10,611 --> 01:04:13,111
This is cut into similar pieces as well.

1550
01:04:14,281 --> 01:04:17,581
Like this. It's good to sort out the edges for this as well.

1551
01:04:17,751 --> 01:04:19,321
Cut them from top to bottom.

1552
01:04:20,121 --> 01:04:22,151
This prevents the potato from becoming mushy.

1553
01:04:22,791 --> 01:04:24,550
Why are the potatoes put into water?

1554
01:04:24,551 --> 01:04:26,090
Because of enzymatic browning,

1555
01:04:26,091 --> 01:04:28,791
potatoes change in color when met with metal or air.

1556
01:04:28,991 --> 01:04:30,390
- It's the same for this. - That was a great question.

1557
01:04:30,391 --> 01:04:31,590
- A sharp question. - That was very sharp.

1558
01:04:31,591 --> 01:04:32,630
For that reason,

1559
01:04:32,631 --> 01:04:34,961
it's best to slice it right after it's prepared.

1560
01:04:36,171 --> 01:04:39,200
You don't press down when slicing these.

1561
01:04:39,201 --> 01:04:40,740
Pressing down will bring out the juice.

1562
01:04:40,741 --> 01:04:42,021
You will lose all of the flavor.

1563
01:04:42,071 --> 01:04:44,070
Use the push cutting technique.

1564
01:04:44,071 --> 01:04:46,711
- You must use the blade. - They must be round pieces.

1565
01:04:47,311 --> 01:04:49,580
There are many details that must be learned...

1566
01:04:49,581 --> 01:04:52,210
beyond just the recipe.

1567
01:04:52,211 --> 01:04:53,851
When cutting vegetables,

1568
01:04:54,321 --> 01:04:57,890
there are many different methods of cutting them.

1569
01:04:57,891 --> 01:04:59,290
It's different for each dish.

1570
01:04:59,291 --> 01:05:02,561
It's hard to remember all of those details.

1571
01:05:02,761 --> 01:05:04,090
It looks easy from afar,

1572
01:05:04,091 --> 01:05:05,630
- It's never easy. - but it's hard once you try.

1573
01:05:05,631 --> 01:05:07,460
When you're done with this, the water will start boiling.

1574
01:05:07,461 --> 01:05:09,400
That is when you prepare the chicken.

1575
01:05:09,401 --> 01:05:11,930
Remove the blood that is just on the outside.

1576
01:05:11,931 --> 01:05:13,800
When you open it up, you'll find the innards.

1577
01:05:13,801 --> 01:05:15,201
You must remove the innards.

1578
01:05:15,601 --> 01:05:18,911
There's a hard part in the middle. Go around it.

1579
01:05:20,341 --> 01:05:21,841
It looks easy from afar.

1580
01:05:22,141 --> 01:05:23,950
There's a bone right here.

1581
01:05:23,951 --> 01:05:25,750
It will be hard to cut when you place the knife here.

1582
01:05:25,751 --> 01:05:28,880
That is why you go around it. These parts will be removed later.

1583
01:05:28,881 --> 01:05:29,951
Like this.

1584
01:05:30,451 --> 01:05:32,821
It will be cut easily like this.

1585
01:05:32,961 --> 01:05:33,990
Remove the cartilage.

1586
01:05:33,991 --> 01:05:36,151
It looks like he has missed quite a few parts already.

1587
01:05:37,691 --> 01:05:38,961
It's all set.

1588
01:05:39,061 --> 01:05:40,731
To the boiling water...

1589
01:05:41,601 --> 01:05:44,230
In Korean cuisine, we call it "splashing" in the water.

1590
01:05:44,231 --> 01:05:46,941
Once it starts boiling, the impurities rise.

1591
01:05:48,171 --> 01:05:50,170
(Super focused)

1592
01:05:50,171 --> 01:05:51,571
Now, add onions and...

1593
01:05:51,941 --> 01:05:53,041
This is for one serving.

1594
01:05:53,681 --> 01:05:55,440
Now, let's wrap it up.

1595
01:05:55,441 --> 01:05:56,841
- It looks good. - It must be good.

1596
01:05:58,051 --> 01:05:59,681
- It looks good. - Yes, it will be good.

1597
01:06:00,721 --> 01:06:02,281
- Add it, and that's all. - All right.

1598
01:06:02,421 --> 01:06:03,721
That's it.

1599
01:06:05,051 --> 01:06:07,361
(Woo Il is back to his station.)

1600
01:06:08,421 --> 01:06:11,231
(Checking the recipe once again)

1601
01:06:12,391 --> 01:06:14,000
Today's menu is too hard.

1602
01:06:14,001 --> 01:06:15,800
I think it'll be harder if you cook there.

1603
01:06:15,801 --> 01:06:16,831
It is hard.

1604
01:06:17,231 --> 01:06:19,371
(Let's start for now.)

1605
01:06:20,441 --> 01:06:22,571
If you miss a step, then it'll get messed up.

1606
01:06:23,411 --> 01:06:26,071
(Shall I begin?)

1607
01:06:26,741 --> 01:06:27,981
(Grabbing the potato first)

1608
01:06:28,711 --> 01:06:30,551
(Determined)

1609
01:06:32,081 --> 01:06:35,881
(He begins to peel it without hesitation.)

1610
01:06:38,091 --> 01:06:40,791
(Focused)

1611
01:06:41,421 --> 01:06:43,430
(Next, he prepares dried chili, )

1612
01:06:43,431 --> 01:06:45,630
(shiitake mushroom, )

1613
01:06:45,631 --> 01:06:47,561
(carrots.)

1614
01:06:48,701 --> 01:06:50,330
He's working so hard.

1615
01:06:50,331 --> 01:06:51,331
(Woo Il prepares the vegetables quite skillfully.)

1616
01:06:51,332 --> 01:06:52,671
Actually, I...

1617
01:06:53,401 --> 01:06:56,741
I did cook a little when I was in the military.

1618
01:06:57,311 --> 01:06:58,710
I cooked for a group of 30 soldiers...

1619
01:06:58,711 --> 01:07:00,780
for 6 months. I made all 3 meals every day...

1620
01:07:00,781 --> 01:07:02,880
and fed them.

1621
01:07:02,881 --> 01:07:03,910
(Dignified)

1622
01:07:03,911 --> 01:07:06,051
- What is that? - Is he an armed infiltrating agent?

1623
01:07:06,721 --> 01:07:08,357
- In Gangwon Province. - An armed infiltrating agent?

1624
01:07:08,381 --> 01:07:09,981
What was the number for reporting a spy?

1625
01:07:10,091 --> 01:07:13,490
When I was in the military stationed at a GOP,

1626
01:07:13,491 --> 01:07:16,731
I worked as the cook's assistant for about 4 to 5 months.

1627
01:07:17,191 --> 01:07:18,590
Was their evaluation okay?

1628
01:07:18,591 --> 01:07:20,430
- How did they react? - They all enjoyed it.

1629
01:07:20,431 --> 01:07:21,530
- Oh, really? - Yes.

1630
01:07:21,531 --> 01:07:24,101
They said I was really good at cooking.

1631
01:07:24,671 --> 01:07:27,141
They complimented me a lot.

1632
01:07:27,501 --> 01:07:28,600
Yes.

1633
01:07:28,601 --> 01:07:31,671
(Almost Became a Legendary Military Cook)

1634
01:07:32,411 --> 01:07:34,811
(Knife work of a former expertise)

1635
01:07:35,411 --> 01:07:38,381
(Burning with confidence)

1636
01:07:40,281 --> 01:07:43,291
(He easily prepared the basic ingredients.)

1637
01:07:43,751 --> 01:07:45,721
(Next, it's time to make a sauce.)

1638
01:07:46,761 --> 01:07:49,931
Getting the right seasoning and thickness is really hard.

1639
01:07:52,031 --> 01:07:54,071
(He looks like a master from the look on his face.)

1640
01:07:56,271 --> 01:07:58,901
(Doing it on his own in order)

1641
01:07:59,541 --> 01:08:00,700
(He quickly finishes making a sauce for seasoning.)

1642
01:08:00,701 --> 01:08:03,410
It's really convenient if you remember how to make seasoning.

1643
01:08:03,411 --> 01:08:05,470
For the Korean Cuisine Culinary Technician Certification Exam,

1644
01:08:05,471 --> 01:08:07,410
- it goes by s, s, g, g, p, s, s. - That's right.

1645
01:08:07,411 --> 01:08:09,081
- Oh, right. - The basic seasoning.

1646
01:08:09,211 --> 01:08:11,311
It's also the order in terms of amount.

1647
01:08:11,451 --> 01:08:12,750
They memorize it like chemical symbols.

1648
01:08:12,751 --> 01:08:14,381
I often use s, s, v.

1649
01:08:14,921 --> 01:08:16,951
- Vinegar? - Soy sauce, sugar, vinegar.

1650
01:08:17,291 --> 01:08:18,450
That's all I use.

1651
01:08:18,451 --> 01:08:20,091
And I never taste my food when I cook.

1652
01:08:20,761 --> 01:08:22,120
- Why not? - Only cowards do.

1653
01:08:22,121 --> 01:08:23,230
That's right.

1654
01:08:23,231 --> 01:08:25,991
You can't taste your food when you take the exam.

1655
01:08:26,231 --> 01:08:28,201
- It shows how skilled you are. - That's right.

1656
01:08:28,961 --> 01:08:30,500
- I was already practicing it. - Your points get deducted?

1657
01:08:30,501 --> 01:08:31,930
- That's right. - I've already realized it.

1658
01:08:31,931 --> 01:08:33,371
(Mysterious)

1659
01:08:33,871 --> 01:08:36,351
(Woo Il has already made seasoning after prepping vegetables.)

1660
01:08:37,041 --> 01:08:38,141
What should I do next?

1661
01:08:43,411 --> 01:08:44,651
Gosh, this is the hard part.

1662
01:08:47,251 --> 01:08:48,650
(It's time to prepare the chicken.)

1663
01:08:48,651 --> 01:08:49,891
This is the hard one.

1664
01:08:52,551 --> 01:08:53,891
It looked easy when she did it.

1665
01:08:54,361 --> 01:08:55,620
(Looking at the chicken)

1666
01:08:55,621 --> 01:08:57,231
(Looking around)

1667
01:08:57,791 --> 01:08:58,830
It feels like...

1668
01:08:58,831 --> 01:09:00,631
- he'd take a bite as is. - A bite like this.

1669
01:09:01,061 --> 01:09:02,130
The backdrop will change...

1670
01:09:02,131 --> 01:09:03,651
- to a mountain. - After it changes...

1671
01:09:04,071 --> 01:09:06,701
Like gnawing the meat without your master knowing.

1672
01:09:07,671 --> 01:09:09,571
- After getting flogged. - "You brat!"

1673
01:09:09,811 --> 01:09:11,241
"I'm sorry, my lord."

1674
01:09:11,571 --> 01:09:13,341
"I was too starved."

1675
01:09:13,541 --> 01:09:14,711
(It suits him so well.)

1676
01:09:14,911 --> 01:09:16,310
(Like a servant who stole a chicken behind his master)

1677
01:09:16,311 --> 01:09:18,551
(Snooping around)

1678
01:09:19,251 --> 01:09:21,921
(His classmates are proceeding smoothly.)

1679
01:09:22,581 --> 01:09:24,891
(Skillfully preparing the drumsticks)

1680
01:09:26,921 --> 01:09:28,290
They all knew how to handle it.

1681
01:09:28,291 --> 01:09:30,131
(They're all so good.)

1682
01:09:32,231 --> 01:09:34,860
(Lifting the knife after plucking up his courage)

1683
01:09:34,861 --> 01:09:36,331
When I was about to face it,

1684
01:09:36,771 --> 01:09:38,870
- I guess you can cut it randomly. - I didn't know what to do.

1685
01:09:38,871 --> 01:09:40,171
I was lost.

1686
01:09:41,241 --> 01:09:42,301
That's right.

1687
01:09:42,741 --> 01:09:44,011
Should I open its belly?

1688
01:09:44,711 --> 01:09:47,440
That's right. Make the chicken breast face up.

1689
01:09:47,441 --> 01:09:48,911
Or does it face down?

1690
01:09:49,411 --> 01:09:50,810
I think you should put it upside down.

1691
01:09:50,811 --> 01:09:52,950
- The middle... - The neck should face down, right?

1692
01:09:52,951 --> 01:09:54,697
It doesn't matter if you put it the other way around.

1693
01:09:54,721 --> 01:09:55,880
Cut it like this.

1694
01:09:55,881 --> 01:09:57,290
That part must be soft, so it should be cut well.

1695
01:09:57,291 --> 01:09:58,950
- Just cut the middle. - I can just cut it like this?

1696
01:09:58,951 --> 01:10:00,560
Cut it and spread it like a butterfly.

1697
01:10:00,561 --> 01:10:01,821
Be careful. It's slippery.

1698
01:10:05,191 --> 01:10:06,330
(Let's go.)

1699
01:10:06,331 --> 01:10:07,430
- Like this. - Gosh, that's unstable.

1700
01:10:07,431 --> 01:10:08,930
If you put your knife in like this, it slips.

1701
01:10:08,931 --> 01:10:09,971
Put it in like this.

1702
01:10:11,231 --> 01:10:12,730
(The knife bounces out without penetrating.)

1703
01:10:12,731 --> 01:10:14,940
Normally, the tips of knives are really sharp,

1704
01:10:14,941 --> 01:10:17,671
so if you put the tip in first... It's sharp.

1705
01:10:17,841 --> 01:10:19,740
- You have to stab it first. - Yes. Then it's easier.

1706
01:10:19,741 --> 01:10:20,841
If it doesn't cut easily.

1707
01:10:21,381 --> 01:10:22,641
You should've told me.

1708
01:10:24,511 --> 01:10:25,880
- There's a bone. - That's right.

1709
01:10:25,881 --> 01:10:28,181
- Use your strength. Yes. - Oh, at once like this?

1710
01:10:28,521 --> 01:10:30,121
Yes, so it can spread.

1711
01:10:30,991 --> 01:10:33,860
Once it spreads open, you'll find the backbone.

1712
01:10:33,861 --> 01:10:35,321
Check the cartilage too.

1713
01:10:36,891 --> 01:10:38,430
(Check the cartilage?)

1714
01:10:38,431 --> 01:10:40,107
- Should I cut it like this? - That's right.

1715
01:10:40,131 --> 01:10:41,361
Otherwise, you cut it wrong.

1716
01:10:41,801 --> 01:10:43,231
Where is the cartilage?

1717
01:10:43,431 --> 01:10:44,900
Try putting in your thumb.

1718
01:10:44,901 --> 01:10:47,240
- This way? - You'll feel it in here.

1719
01:10:47,241 --> 01:10:49,241
- Where should I put it in? - Over here.

1720
01:10:49,741 --> 01:10:50,770
The sunken part.

1721
01:10:50,771 --> 01:10:51,940
- Oh, here? - Here.

1722
01:10:51,941 --> 01:10:53,901
- It's around the armpit. - Yes, put it in there.

1723
01:10:54,011 --> 01:10:55,941
Try slashing it as you cut.

1724
01:10:56,611 --> 01:10:58,510
(The armpit...)

1725
01:10:58,511 --> 01:11:00,480
(The armpit...)

1726
01:11:00,481 --> 01:11:02,320
- So... - I should give it a try.

1727
01:11:02,321 --> 01:11:04,150
She said it was the sunken part.

1728
01:11:04,151 --> 01:11:05,350
That's where the cartilage is.

1729
01:11:05,351 --> 01:11:07,491
- But every part is sunken. - That's right.

1730
01:11:07,591 --> 01:11:08,660
It's all sunken.

1731
01:11:08,661 --> 01:11:11,861
If you bend your finger, you can see that this is the cartilage.

1732
01:11:12,031 --> 01:11:13,700
If you bend it backward...

1733
01:11:13,701 --> 01:11:15,677
- Bend it backward, and... - Oh, bend the leg backward?

1734
01:11:15,701 --> 01:11:17,801
Then you can remove the bones completely.

1735
01:11:18,271 --> 01:11:19,477
Oh, you have to bend it backward.

1736
01:11:19,501 --> 01:11:22,441
Then you just need to cut the boneless part.

1737
01:11:22,741 --> 01:11:24,411
That's really easy to understand.

1738
01:11:26,241 --> 01:11:28,141
(I can't find the cartilage.)

1739
01:11:28,511 --> 01:11:29,881
You're checking its pulse.

1740
01:11:31,581 --> 01:11:32,851
You're massaging the chicken.

1741
01:11:35,221 --> 01:11:37,120
- You do that for an upset stomach. - Can't it digest well?

1742
01:11:37,121 --> 01:11:38,320
- That's right. - Why do you keep pressing that?

1743
01:11:38,321 --> 01:11:39,797
The chicken must have an upset stomach.

1744
01:11:39,821 --> 01:11:41,090
It must have a sudden indigestion.

1745
01:11:41,091 --> 01:11:42,697
You're the only one who's like Heo Jun there.

1746
01:11:42,721 --> 01:11:43,930
Softly.

1747
01:11:43,931 --> 01:11:45,331
(Woo Il's hand is healing.)

1748
01:11:46,331 --> 01:11:48,760
(Woo Il was massaging the chicken with all his effort.)

1749
01:11:48,761 --> 01:11:50,571
(Glancing over)

1750
01:11:52,201 --> 01:11:53,741
(Glancing over again)

1751
01:11:54,101 --> 01:11:56,671
(Excuse me, ma'am...)

1752
01:11:57,571 --> 01:11:58,941
This one...

1753
01:11:59,941 --> 01:12:01,710
- This one... - It must be connected there.

1754
01:12:01,711 --> 01:12:03,010
- Where is the cartilage? - Between the thigh meat.

1755
01:12:03,011 --> 01:12:04,331
I don't think it has a cartilage.

1756
01:12:04,511 --> 01:12:05,581
It does.

1757
01:12:07,081 --> 01:12:08,350
- Is this it? - If you press it hard,

1758
01:12:08,351 --> 01:12:10,221
it could be crushed, so use a knife next time.

1759
01:12:11,721 --> 01:12:12,820
That's right. It's like that.

1760
01:12:12,821 --> 01:12:14,260
Cut the thigh separately.

1761
01:12:14,261 --> 01:12:15,520
The backbone can be cut.

1762
01:12:15,521 --> 01:12:16,690
- Oh, here? - Yes.

1763
01:12:16,691 --> 01:12:19,331
Remove the fat around the neck.

1764
01:12:19,461 --> 01:12:21,261
- Then slice it. - It's not easy.

1765
01:12:22,001 --> 01:12:23,347
It's really hard when it's your first time.

1766
01:12:23,371 --> 01:12:24,531
It must be really hard.

1767
01:12:25,231 --> 01:12:27,271
(Struggling)

1768
01:12:28,641 --> 01:12:30,510
It takes forever to prepare the chicken.

1769
01:12:30,511 --> 01:12:32,311
(He took a long time to prepare the chicken.)

1770
01:12:32,741 --> 01:12:34,411
(Glancing over)

1771
01:12:35,111 --> 01:12:38,351
(Hold on. The others are...)

1772
01:12:39,451 --> 01:12:43,191
(They're already moving into the final phase.)

1773
01:12:44,351 --> 01:12:45,550
(Woo Il has only the cleaned chicken on his table.)

1774
01:12:45,551 --> 01:12:47,891
(Anxious)

1775
01:12:48,521 --> 01:12:52,461
You feel really anxious if you're the only one falling behind.

1776
01:12:53,531 --> 01:12:55,801
(He anxiously checks the recipe.)

1777
01:12:56,971 --> 01:12:58,571
This is when speed matters.

1778
01:12:59,671 --> 01:13:01,701
(Boiling the chicken)

1779
01:13:01,941 --> 01:13:04,710
Pour out the water.

1780
01:13:04,711 --> 01:13:07,311
(Pour out the water used to boil the chicken for the first time.)

1781
01:13:07,341 --> 01:13:09,311
(Entering the chicken in the fresh water)

1782
01:13:10,051 --> 01:13:11,780
(Quickly pouring the seasoning)

1783
01:13:11,781 --> 01:13:13,920
While jjimdak is being cooked,

1784
01:13:13,921 --> 01:13:15,481
we'll make a rolled omelet.

1785
01:13:16,121 --> 01:13:18,891
(We're making a rolled omelet now?)

1786
01:13:20,021 --> 01:13:21,761
Everyone was done and waiting for me.

1787
01:13:22,521 --> 01:13:23,631
Oh, no.

1788
01:13:24,861 --> 01:13:27,331
Mine wasn't ready,

1789
01:13:27,661 --> 01:13:29,401
but I had to make a rolled omelet,

1790
01:13:29,501 --> 01:13:30,930
so I broke down.

1791
01:13:30,931 --> 01:13:32,631
(My jjimdak isn't ready yet.)

1792
01:13:33,301 --> 01:13:36,401
(Let's put everything in for now.)

1793
01:13:37,241 --> 01:13:39,941
Now, please come to the front again.

1794
01:13:40,641 --> 01:13:42,881
(Trudging after adding the vegetables to the pot)

1795
01:13:43,311 --> 01:13:45,150
We'll now make steamed mussels and rice cake...

1796
01:13:45,151 --> 01:13:46,181
and a rolled omelet.

1797
01:13:47,081 --> 01:13:48,997
I've never heard of steamed mussels and rice cake.

1798
01:13:49,021 --> 01:13:51,651
What we need for the rolled omelet is five eggs...

1799
01:13:51,821 --> 01:13:53,637
and three different vegetables that are minced.

1800
01:13:53,661 --> 01:13:56,190
Grab this and roll it.

1801
01:13:56,191 --> 01:13:58,761
Grab it diagonally and flip it.

1802
01:13:59,431 --> 01:14:00,760
Make the pan bounce too.

1803
01:14:00,761 --> 01:14:01,831
(Like this)

1804
01:14:02,461 --> 01:14:05,001
Now, to the steamed mussels and rice cake...

1805
01:14:05,231 --> 01:14:06,471
Be careful not to get splashed.

1806
01:14:06,741 --> 01:14:08,341
(I see.)

1807
01:14:08,741 --> 01:14:10,270
As for mussels, you can find aquatic plants in them.

1808
01:14:10,271 --> 01:14:11,941
Just remove these only.

1809
01:14:12,141 --> 01:14:13,241
Then rub it.

1810
01:14:15,881 --> 01:14:17,050
There's no soup for this dish.

1811
01:14:17,051 --> 01:14:18,550
- There's no soup. - Oh, it's like that.

1812
01:14:18,551 --> 01:14:20,521
It's like tteokbokki.

1813
01:14:20,581 --> 01:14:22,121
You'll try it now.

1814
01:14:22,881 --> 01:14:24,050
(Preparing to make a rolled omelet)

1815
01:14:24,051 --> 01:14:25,691
Pouring it in the pan is easy.

1816
01:14:25,991 --> 01:14:27,390
- The next part is the hard one. - Rolling it is hard.

1817
01:14:27,391 --> 01:14:29,791
- Rolling is the difficult part. - Rolling requires a skill.

1818
01:14:29,931 --> 01:14:31,761
(Let's go for it.)

1819
01:14:33,301 --> 01:14:34,830
(Carefully pouring it into the pan)

1820
01:14:34,831 --> 01:14:36,630
- Controlling the fire is important. - That's right.

1821
01:14:36,631 --> 01:14:38,300
You need to do it well. Make it thin.

1822
01:14:38,301 --> 01:14:39,330
The amount you pour...

1823
01:14:39,331 --> 01:14:40,800
- must be important too. - Yes, to make it even.

1824
01:14:40,801 --> 01:14:42,841
- You can't pour too much. - All right.

1825
01:14:44,341 --> 01:14:45,711
Go back and forth horizontally.

1826
01:14:46,171 --> 01:14:48,410
Tilt it a little and roll it. One, two, three.

1827
01:14:48,411 --> 01:14:49,651
(It's time to roll the omelet.)

1828
01:14:49,881 --> 01:14:52,051
(Now, it's the real thing.)

1829
01:14:52,281 --> 01:14:53,780
Give it a try. One, two, three.

1830
01:14:53,781 --> 01:14:55,220
I think that part will tear. Oh, no.

1831
01:14:55,221 --> 01:14:57,420
- You did that at once. - He'll make a huge omelet today.

1832
01:14:57,421 --> 01:14:59,097
One, two, three. I think that part will tear.

1833
01:14:59,121 --> 01:15:00,220
Oh, no.

1834
01:15:00,221 --> 01:15:01,891
(Folding the omelet without holding back)

1835
01:15:02,891 --> 01:15:04,161
It's like a crepe.

1836
01:15:05,831 --> 01:15:07,761
Yes, like that. Then push it.

1837
01:15:09,661 --> 01:15:11,770
(Rolling it with all his body)

1838
01:15:11,771 --> 01:15:12,801
Wait.

1839
01:15:14,441 --> 01:15:17,041
(In tatters)

1840
01:15:17,471 --> 01:15:19,310
(Should I just give up cooking?)

1841
01:15:19,311 --> 01:15:20,710
You need to put the chopsticks diagonally...

1842
01:15:20,711 --> 01:15:22,110
- so it wouldn't tear. - That's correct.

1843
01:15:22,111 --> 01:15:23,411
You're indeed good.

1844
01:15:24,011 --> 01:15:25,880
She's a chef.

1845
01:15:25,881 --> 01:15:27,951
- Si Hyeon is a chef. - I know.

1846
01:15:28,181 --> 01:15:29,550
The instructor said the exact same thing.

1847
01:15:29,551 --> 01:15:31,650
Put the chopsticks diagonally so it wouldn't have a hole.

1848
01:15:31,651 --> 01:15:33,190
- Diagonally. - That's right.

1849
01:15:33,191 --> 01:15:34,391
(I see.)

1850
01:15:34,721 --> 01:15:36,560
I think it's too much for me to make three dishes.

1851
01:15:36,561 --> 01:15:38,390
I think it's too hard.

1852
01:15:38,391 --> 01:15:40,200
Making three dishes is too hectic.

1853
01:15:40,201 --> 01:15:41,201
(Agreeing)

1854
01:15:41,261 --> 01:15:43,600
Since you need to wash the dishes too,

1855
01:15:43,601 --> 01:15:44,901
finish the dishes...

1856
01:15:45,631 --> 01:15:46,741
until 6:30pm.

1857
01:15:47,271 --> 01:15:48,801
What? I haven't made that yet.

1858
01:15:49,841 --> 01:15:53,511
There's another class afterward,

1859
01:15:53,711 --> 01:15:55,611
so we need to clean up and leave.

1860
01:15:56,181 --> 01:15:58,181
(They have only 15 minutes.)

1861
01:15:59,481 --> 01:16:01,320
(The other classmates are making the last dish already.)

1862
01:16:01,321 --> 01:16:03,220
While I was making a rolled omelet,

1863
01:16:03,221 --> 01:16:04,721
the rest of them were done.

1864
01:16:05,921 --> 01:16:08,261
(Glancing over)

1865
01:16:08,761 --> 01:16:10,361
It's okay to do the last roll well only.

1866
01:16:12,331 --> 01:16:13,360
(It's his last chance.)

1867
01:16:13,361 --> 01:16:15,000
As long as you do the final roll well...

1868
01:16:15,001 --> 01:16:16,401
- Yes, the shape... - The last one.

1869
01:16:16,901 --> 01:16:18,870
One, two, three. Flip it lightly.

1870
01:16:18,871 --> 01:16:20,301
Yes. You saved it.

1871
01:16:22,071 --> 01:16:23,270
- You saved it. - Nice!

1872
01:16:23,271 --> 01:16:26,371
(He saved the rolled omelet with all his body.)

1873
01:16:28,041 --> 01:16:29,641
You can just do the last roll well.

1874
01:16:30,981 --> 01:16:32,651
(Feeling proud)

1875
01:16:32,751 --> 01:16:35,351
Please wrap up your dishes within ten minutes.

1876
01:16:35,821 --> 01:16:38,421
(In 10 minutes?)

1877
01:16:39,291 --> 01:16:41,572
You haven't started making steamed mussels and rice cake.

1878
01:16:41,700 --> 01:16:44,310
Please wrap up your dishes within ten minutes.

1879
01:16:44,810 --> 01:16:48,110
(In 10 minutes?)

1880
01:16:48,240 --> 01:16:50,521
You haven't started making steamed mussels and rice cake.

1881
01:16:51,580 --> 01:16:53,179
(The other classmates are wrapping up already.)

1882
01:16:53,180 --> 01:16:55,419
- She looks like an expert. - Right. She's good.

1883
01:16:55,420 --> 01:16:58,350
(The other classmates are wrapping up already.)

1884
01:17:01,490 --> 01:17:04,430
(He hasn't even started on the next dish.)

1885
01:17:05,790 --> 01:17:08,600
(Woo Il is feeling rushed.)

1886
01:17:09,530 --> 01:17:11,500
- Oh, no! - What is it?

1887
01:17:12,500 --> 01:17:15,570
(Splash)

1888
01:17:15,840 --> 01:17:16,840
The bag...

1889
01:17:16,841 --> 01:17:19,140
(He put the whole thing in the bag as he felt rushed.)

1890
01:17:20,880 --> 01:17:24,480
(Flapping)

1891
01:17:25,550 --> 01:17:27,120
(Upset)

1892
01:17:28,150 --> 01:17:29,619
(As he doesn't have time, )

1893
01:17:29,620 --> 01:17:31,690
(he quickly moves on to the next step.)

1894
01:17:32,350 --> 01:17:34,190
How do I clean mussels?

1895
01:17:34,520 --> 01:17:36,120
Just rub it hard.

1896
01:17:36,460 --> 01:17:38,260
You need to remove the threads.

1897
01:17:39,230 --> 01:17:41,000
(Busy)

1898
01:17:41,330 --> 01:17:42,460
He's working faster.

1899
01:17:42,830 --> 01:17:44,500
I know. I felt even more rushed.

1900
01:17:45,370 --> 01:17:46,940
(Taking out the boiled rice cakes)

1901
01:17:47,800 --> 01:17:49,716
(Putting it in the bowl right away as he's rushed)

1902
01:17:49,740 --> 01:17:51,339
Now, please wrap up...

1903
01:17:51,340 --> 01:17:53,709
- in five minutes. - It drove me crazy.

1904
01:17:53,710 --> 01:17:54,979
- Five minutes? - Yes.

1905
01:17:54,980 --> 01:17:56,280
(This is bad.)

1906
01:17:57,050 --> 01:18:00,080
(Others are already washing their dishes.)

1907
01:18:01,120 --> 01:18:03,079
(Thinking hard)

1908
01:18:03,080 --> 01:18:04,619
He's become serious.

1909
01:18:04,620 --> 01:18:05,620
(Looking around)

1910
01:18:06,020 --> 01:18:08,360
(Sighing)

1911
01:18:09,690 --> 01:18:11,530
No way. This won't work.

1912
01:18:14,130 --> 01:18:16,800
(He checks the ingredients.)

1913
01:18:18,200 --> 01:18:19,200
It won't work.

1914
01:18:19,201 --> 01:18:20,921
(Heading somewhere after making a decision)

1915
01:18:24,110 --> 01:18:25,810
(Tearing)

1916
01:18:28,840 --> 01:18:30,509
You can tear that so well.

1917
01:18:30,510 --> 01:18:31,750
He's the best at that.

1918
01:18:33,520 --> 01:18:36,350
(Returning with the plastic bags he tore so well)

1919
01:18:39,850 --> 01:18:42,050
(He decides to pack the steamed mussels and rice cake.)

1920
01:18:42,460 --> 01:18:45,090
(Putting the hot rice cakes straight into the plastic bag)

1921
01:18:45,430 --> 01:18:46,660
(Taking it)

1922
01:18:47,230 --> 01:18:48,659
(Pouring)

1923
01:18:48,660 --> 01:18:49,660
What?

1924
01:18:50,270 --> 01:18:52,270
- You threw away the broth? - That's...

1925
01:18:52,400 --> 01:18:54,070
That was mussel soup.

1926
01:18:56,170 --> 01:18:58,240
(After throwing away the mussel broth, )

1927
01:18:59,910 --> 01:19:03,380
(he frantically packed the jjimdak as well.)

1928
01:19:04,810 --> 01:19:06,780
(Squeaky clean)

1929
01:19:07,480 --> 01:19:09,220
(I should do the dishes quickly, at least.)

1930
01:19:11,790 --> 01:19:12,819
(The classmate who's done goes home first.)

1931
01:19:12,820 --> 01:19:14,219
- She's the ace. - Oh, no.

1932
01:19:14,220 --> 01:19:16,660
- She seemed extraordinary. - She's leaving.

1933
01:19:17,690 --> 01:19:22,060
Everyone else was leaving when I was washing the dishes.

1934
01:19:23,830 --> 01:19:25,869
So I felt more rushed.

1935
01:19:25,870 --> 01:19:27,770
(I felt rushed.)

1936
01:19:30,140 --> 01:19:33,740
(Woo Il quickly wrapped it up.)

1937
01:19:34,380 --> 01:19:37,279
- Great work. - Good job.

1938
01:19:37,280 --> 01:19:38,579
- Have a good day. - See you next time.

1939
01:19:38,580 --> 01:19:40,780
- See you. Great job. - Bye.

1940
01:19:42,720 --> 01:19:46,150
(Sighing)

1941
01:19:47,890 --> 01:19:50,590
(Trudging)

1942
01:19:52,590 --> 01:19:55,029
(It's gotten dark outside already.)

1943
01:19:55,030 --> 01:19:56,059
It must've been tiring.

1944
01:19:56,060 --> 01:19:57,646
- It must've drained you. - I was exhausted.

1945
01:19:57,670 --> 01:19:59,406
You were there for more than three hours, right?

1946
01:19:59,430 --> 01:20:02,000
Three hours and a half to four hours.

1947
01:20:02,740 --> 01:20:04,510
(I worked hard today again.)

1948
01:20:05,510 --> 01:20:06,810
Your day's gone.

1949
01:20:08,080 --> 01:20:11,150
(Opening)

1950
01:20:12,150 --> 01:20:13,150
(Sighing)

1951
01:20:14,580 --> 01:20:17,389
(He trudged back home.)

1952
01:20:17,390 --> 01:20:18,390
(Sighing)

1953
01:20:20,220 --> 01:20:23,060
(Turning on the lights as soon as he's home)

1954
01:20:24,790 --> 01:20:25,959
(He's free from the cooking bag...)

1955
01:20:25,960 --> 01:20:27,129
(that's been weighing down his shoulders.)

1956
01:20:27,130 --> 01:20:28,160
(Sighing)

1957
01:20:32,600 --> 01:20:36,000
(Feeling more fatigue as his tension relaxes)

1958
01:20:37,010 --> 01:20:39,870
(Flopping onto the sofa)

1959
01:20:41,180 --> 01:20:44,749
I think it's harder than working out for three hours.

1960
01:20:44,750 --> 01:20:45,909
- It is exhausting. - That's right.

1961
01:20:45,910 --> 01:20:46,979
Seriously, I...

1962
01:20:46,980 --> 01:20:49,919
Doing something in the field you're not familiar with is not easy.

1963
01:20:49,920 --> 01:20:52,689
But as I learned to cook,

1964
01:20:52,690 --> 01:20:55,690
I kept getting reminded of my mom.

1965
01:20:55,760 --> 01:20:57,859
- That's right. - I thought, "How did she..."

1966
01:20:57,860 --> 01:20:59,660
- "do this all her life?" - That's right.

1967
01:20:59,930 --> 01:21:01,759
You come to realize that as you cook.

1968
01:21:01,760 --> 01:21:03,259
- Seriously. - It could seem like a simple dish,

1969
01:21:03,260 --> 01:21:04,399
but it takes a lot of effort.

1970
01:21:04,400 --> 01:21:05,629
There's no such thing as a simple dish.

1971
01:21:05,630 --> 01:21:07,400
- There's no such thing. - You're right.

1972
01:21:08,170 --> 01:21:11,270
(Woo Il is worn out and exhausted like a sock with a hole.)

1973
01:21:13,480 --> 01:21:15,910
(Gosh, let's collect myself.)

1974
01:21:16,850 --> 01:21:19,810
(Dragging his worn-out self to the kitchen)

1975
01:21:20,920 --> 01:21:22,450
(Bumping his head)

1976
01:21:23,050 --> 01:21:26,190
(He doesn't even have the energy to feel the pain.)

1977
01:21:26,350 --> 01:21:28,420
(Opening his fridge)

1978
01:21:29,160 --> 01:21:33,930
(Filled with leftover food)

1979
01:21:34,100 --> 01:21:36,059
(He's saved even the smallest bit.)

1980
01:21:36,060 --> 01:21:37,200
It looks exactly like mine.

1981
01:21:37,770 --> 01:21:39,269
I think food is like clothes.

1982
01:21:39,270 --> 01:21:41,299
That's what food means to me.

1983
01:21:41,300 --> 01:21:44,440
I'd think, "Wouldn't I have a bite of this one day?"

1984
01:21:44,640 --> 01:21:47,780
That's why I freeze everything and keep them in the fridge.

1985
01:21:47,810 --> 01:21:48,879
That's true.

1986
01:21:48,880 --> 01:21:51,679
With the same thought, I had the meat in the freezer,

1987
01:21:51,680 --> 01:21:53,150
and I got a stomachache.

1988
01:21:53,680 --> 01:21:55,479
- Just don't do it. - For real though,

1989
01:21:55,480 --> 01:21:57,919
I don't know what I froze in the freezer.

1990
01:21:57,920 --> 01:21:59,019
You shouldn't do it.

1991
01:21:59,020 --> 01:22:01,019
- You get a stomachache. - So I realize what it is...

1992
01:22:01,020 --> 01:22:02,060
after I microwave it.

1993
01:22:02,590 --> 01:22:04,459
- To learn the original form of it? - Yes.

1994
01:22:04,460 --> 01:22:05,859
- A blind dish. - A microwave is like...

1995
01:22:05,860 --> 01:22:07,260
a time machine for you.

1996
01:22:07,960 --> 01:22:10,330
I've always lived like this,

1997
01:22:10,730 --> 01:22:13,170
but after I started learning to cook, it's been bothering me.

1998
01:22:13,230 --> 01:22:16,099
I kept thinking of cleaning up the fridge.

1999
01:22:16,100 --> 01:22:17,940
I've only had such a thought,

2000
01:22:18,610 --> 01:22:21,239
then I realized I shouldn't push it off any longer.

2001
01:22:21,240 --> 01:22:24,079
"I should clean it up." That's why I started on it.

2002
01:22:24,080 --> 01:22:25,560
(Let's clean up the fridge for real!)

2003
01:22:26,350 --> 01:22:31,290
(Let's take them all out first.)

2004
01:22:33,020 --> 01:22:34,489
(Ingredients keep coming out like a magic treasure jar.)

2005
01:22:34,490 --> 01:22:36,119
I wanted to clean all of them up.

2006
01:22:36,120 --> 01:22:37,759
(Ingredients keep coming out like a magic treasure jar.)

2007
01:22:37,760 --> 01:22:39,990
It's really tiring to clean up a fridge.

2008
01:22:40,400 --> 01:22:42,600
(Piled up)

2009
01:22:43,500 --> 01:22:45,730
(Taking out the plastic cups he's been collecting)

2010
01:22:46,900 --> 01:22:49,240
- He's finally throwing them. - Finally.

2011
01:22:49,800 --> 01:22:52,570
(There were always plastic cups in his fridge.)

2012
01:22:53,440 --> 01:22:57,710
(He drank the leftover coffees from his fridge endless times.)

2013
01:22:57,910 --> 01:23:00,520
(Woo Il has changed.)

2014
01:23:01,020 --> 01:23:03,250
(Pushing the cup in)

2015
01:23:04,550 --> 01:23:06,096
- That's... - Are those two your favorites?

2016
01:23:06,120 --> 01:23:07,990
They're not my favorite,

2017
01:23:08,560 --> 01:23:10,430
but I just couldn't throw out everything.

2018
01:23:11,060 --> 01:23:13,329
Around a week ago,

2019
01:23:13,330 --> 01:23:16,429
a little kid was walking past me in my apartment complex.

2020
01:23:16,430 --> 01:23:18,969
He asked, "Do you really keep the takeout coffees in your fridge..."

2021
01:23:18,970 --> 01:23:20,340
"and keep drinking them?"

2022
01:23:20,700 --> 01:23:22,170
I said, "Yes, I do."

2023
01:23:22,300 --> 01:23:24,139
He went, "That's not good for your health. Don't do that."

2024
01:23:24,140 --> 01:23:25,140
Then he went home.

2025
01:23:25,141 --> 01:23:27,609
- He's smart. - After hearing him,

2026
01:23:27,610 --> 01:23:30,010
I realized I should really throw them out.

2027
01:23:30,880 --> 01:23:32,849
- We've said that about 30 times. - I know. A little kid said that...

2028
01:23:32,850 --> 01:23:34,020
I've said it 40 times.

2029
01:23:34,420 --> 01:23:37,020
I could relate to it more because a kid said it.

2030
01:23:37,420 --> 01:23:39,390
- "Even kids are worried about me." - Yes.

2031
01:23:40,320 --> 01:23:41,760
(Let's see.)

2032
01:23:42,620 --> 01:23:44,289
January, 2026.

2033
01:23:44,290 --> 01:23:45,830
Goodness, it's expired.

2034
01:23:46,030 --> 01:23:48,160
September 9, 2025?

2035
01:23:48,300 --> 01:23:50,500
February 12, 2026.

2036
01:23:51,330 --> 01:23:52,999
(Boldly throwing out the items that are expired)

2037
01:23:53,000 --> 01:23:54,569
You shouldn't have the ingredients that are expired.

2038
01:23:54,570 --> 01:23:56,139
- You shouldn't. - Right. You shouldn't.

2039
01:23:56,140 --> 01:23:57,716
(Boldly throwing out the items that are expired)

2040
01:23:57,740 --> 01:23:59,639
(Let's throw out the old makgeolli too.)

2041
01:23:59,640 --> 01:24:00,680
Makgeolli?

2042
01:24:00,780 --> 01:24:01,810
(Hissing)

2043
01:24:13,390 --> 01:24:14,490
Darn it.

2044
01:24:16,020 --> 01:24:17,059
(The makgeolli exploded like a celebratory champagne.)

2045
01:24:17,060 --> 01:24:18,130
Why is there so much?

2046
01:24:19,760 --> 01:24:21,860
(Oh, no!)

2047
01:24:22,900 --> 01:24:24,470
- Gosh. - Oh, no!

2048
01:24:25,670 --> 01:24:26,730
Darn it.

2049
01:24:27,770 --> 01:24:29,370
(It splashed all over me.)

2050
01:24:29,540 --> 01:24:31,869
It'd have been better to keep it in the fridge.

2051
01:24:31,870 --> 01:24:33,010
Oh, no.

2052
01:24:34,410 --> 01:24:37,380
(He's forced to clean the kitchen as well.)

2053
01:24:38,050 --> 01:24:40,380
(Wiping it clean)

2054
01:24:40,950 --> 01:24:43,250
